{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'hyp-20261004-002-r1s1|104\\\\.26' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl; sed -n '1,100p' formal/checks_si.cfg; sed -n '215,270p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Remove combinational division from EX with a shared iterative divider\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline reaches only 5.442 MHz and its worst Gowin path spans 156\\ncells through forwarding, EX and the ALU. The ALU currently implements\\nsigned and unsigned division and remainder as combinational / and %\\noperators. These unrolled arithmetic networks impose their delay and\\nrouting cost on the whole processor, including cycles executing ordinary\\ninteger instructions. There are no recorded structural attempts or\\nLESSONS.md entries for this fixture. Replacing the divider's spatially\\nunrolled datapath with a sequential unit is the strongest structural\\nopportunity suggested by the available timing and RTL evidence.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline reaches only 5.442 MHz and its worst Gowin path spans 156\\ncells through forwarding, EX and the ALU. The ALU currently implements\\nsigned and unsigned division and remainder as combinational / and %\\noperators. These unrolled arithmetic networks impose their delay and\\nrouting cost on the whole processor, including cycles executing ordinary\\ninteger instructions. There are no recorded structural attempts or\\nLESSONS.md entries for this fixture. Replacing the divider's spatially\\nunrolled datapath with a sequential unit is the strongest structural\\nopportunity suggested by the available timing and RTL evidence.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the single-retirement in-order pipeline and existing combinational\\ninteger ALU and multiplier, but move DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU into one\\nclocked radix-2 restoring divider. Use a registered 32-bit divisor,\\ndividend/quotient shift register and 33-bit partial remainder; perform\\none compare/subtract-and-select step per cycle for 32 iterations.\\nRegister signed magnitude preparation and final quotient/remainder sign\\ncorrection separately so no cycle chains normalization, iteration and\\ncorrection. Sharing quotient and remainder computation eliminates all\\nreal comb\n…[11898 of 14970 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c10147c2810d33a1]…\n ADDI(1, 0, 99)]\n    assert not leaked_insn, f\"skipped instr ADDI x1,99 retired: {leaked_insn}\"\n    # Sanity: the target instruction and the EBREAK do retire, in order.\n    pcs = [r[\"pc\"] for r in rets]\n    assert pcs == [0, 8, 12], f\"unexpected retirement PC sequence: {pcs}\"\n    [r2] = _by_insn(rets, ADDI(2, 0, 42))\n    assert r2[\"rd_wdata\"] == 42\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def jal_writes_pc_plus_4(dut):\n    \"\"\"JAL x1, +8; verify the wrong-path slot does not retire and x1=PC+4.\"\"\"\n    program = [\n        JAL(1, 8),             # PC=0; x1 := 4; jump to PC=8\n        ADDI(2, 0, 99),        # PC=4 — wrong-path; must not retire\n        ADDI(3, 0, 42),        # PC=8 — target\n        EBREAK(),\n    ]\n    rets, _ = await _run(dut, program)\n    pcs = [r[\"pc\"] for r in rets]\n    assert pcs == [0, 8, 12], f\"unexpected retirement PC sequence: {pcs}\"\n    [rj] = _by_insn(rets, JAL(1, 8))\n    assert rj[\"rd\"] == 1\n    assert rj[\"rd_wdata\"] == rj[\"pc\"] + 4\n    [r3] = _by_insn(rets, ADDI(3, 0, 42))\n    assert r3[\"rd_wdata\"] == 42\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
