{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"Implemented retimed bypass control with complete registered ALU selections.\n\nDecode compares the incoming raw source fields with ID/EX's destination and\nEX/MEM's destination, using EX's actual normal/completed-M post-trap write\neligibility and MEM's post-alignment write eligibility. EX/MEM has priority;\nx0 and invalid/trapped non-writers cannot claim either source. These choices\nand the complete four-way one-hot ALU choices capture with ID/EX, including\nvalidity-killed captures, and hold during memory backpressure and M ownership.\nEX forms each ALU input as a parallel masked OR. Ordinary operand bypasses\nremain separate for branches, JALR, stores, and RVFI snapshots. M acceptance\ncontinues to snapshot its selected operands and its completion transfers the\nexisting saved bundle. Pipeline depth, instruction latencies, arithmetic,\nALTOPS formulas, the load-use bubble, register file, bus behavior, and RVFI\nretirement logic are unchanged.\n\nAll existing pipeline assertions were retained. Added reset/selection-hold\nchecks, post-trap producer suppression, competing writers on both operands,\nAUIPC/immediate overrides with raw source aliases, WB-to-ID collisions,\nload-use and retained-WB cases, consecutive M dependencies, and 180 randomized\nADD/ADDI/MUL/XOR operations with loads/stores under instruction/data stalls.\nThe existing tests also cover dependent branches/JALR/stores, redirect-killed\npayloads, M launch/completion, reset during M ownership, exactly-once requests,\nand exact retirement order.\n\nLocal verification on the final RTL: warning-free Verilator lint; all seven\ncocotb suite runners passed, including all 27 expanded pipeline tests; fast\nALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks (zero failures and zero fix attempts);\nselftest cosim passed with and without random stalls; stalled canonical\nCoreMark CRC validation passed. CoreMark markers were 16,152 and 4,789,988,\ngiving exactly 4,773,836 bracketed cycles for 10 iterations, with all canonical\nCRCs, the validation literal, and no out-of-bounds accesses. The final binary\nalso passed run_coremark_ipc with the same 4,773,836 bracketed cycles.\n\nGowin placement options 0/1/2 measured 65.364/67.190/67.190 MHz, median\n67.190 MHz. Fitness from the vendor median and canonical bracketed cycles is\n140.74635 iterations/sec, approximately 8.39% above the 61.988 MHz champion\nat unchanged cycles, exceeding the 135.8231 acceptance threshold. Resources:\n3,057 logic cells, 818 registers, eight LUT-RAMs, six BSRAMs, and two DSPs.\nOption 0's worst path is 20.593 ns and 12 cells from the register file through\ndecode/EX/ALU into data memory.\n\nNo architectural deviations. Producer eligibility explicitly includes the\nbackend transfer enable (!stall / !hold_wb), so a held producer does not claim\na new transfer; decode's selections retain their prior values during those\nholds. ALU selection registers reset to zero with the empty ID/EX bundle and\nbecome one-hot on the first payload capture. Physical timing differs slightly\nfrom the advisory trial: the median is 0.193 MHz higher and uses 42 more logic\ncells. Remaining timing concerns are selection fanout, post-trap eligibility,\nand the ALU/register-file data paths. The real multiplier/divider were\nunchanged; formal-deep was not rerun. The orchestrator's independent gates\nand fitness evaluation remain the acceptance authority.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
