{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' formal/checks-deep.cfg\"","aggregated_output":"[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Substitutes M-ext mul/div/rem with\n# simpler XOR-of-add/sub formulas so bitwuzla solves them inside the\n# 20-step BMC budget (rvfi.rst §7.6 \"Alternative arithmetic operations\").\n#\n# IMPORTANT — what this DOES and DOES NOT prove:\n#   ✓ proves: M-ext bypassing, operand routing (rs1/rs2 -> unit -> rd),\n#             stall/forwarding behavior on mul/div, all I-base arithmetic\n#             and trap discipline.\n#   ✗ does NOT prove: the actual MUL/DIV/REM arithmetic is correct.\n#     The DUT and the spec both implement the same XOR substitute under\n#     this define, so a wrong real multiplier can still pass formal here.\n#\n# M-extension arithmetic correctness is covered separately by:\n#   1. test/test_alu.py — 30+ vectors per M-ext op, exact bit results.\n#   2. cosim against test/cosim/reference.py — every retired MUL/DIV/REM\n#      diff'd field-by-field.\n#   3. `make formal-deep` — same checks without ALTOPS. SLOW (~hours per\n#      M-ext check) but proves real arithmetic. Run periodically, not in\n#      the per-iteration orchestrator loop.\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\n# A vacuous suite (nothing ever retires) would fail this — no symbolic\n# trace can make rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap go true.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core_pkg.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/alu.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/decoder.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/imm_gen.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/reg_file.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/if_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/id_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/ex_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/mem_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wb_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/hazard_unit.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/forward_unit.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_38", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '200,420p' tools/eval/fpga.py\"", "aggregated_output": "        return {**r, 'perturbation': k}\n    return await asyncio.gather(*(one(k, s) for k, s in pairs))\n\ndef run_coremark_ipc(worktree: str, sim_bin: str | None = None, env: dict | None = None) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Run CoreMark on Verilator sim, return {iter_per_cycle, completed, cycles, iterations}.\n    Completion is only trusted when the simulation retired an ebreak — otherwise the\n    benchmark hit maxcycles without completing and the cycle count is meaningless.\"\"\"\n    worktree_path = Path(worktree).resolve()\n    if sim_bin is None:\n        sim_bin = str(worktree_path / \"test/cosim/obj_dir/cosim_sim\")\n    elf     = str(worktree_path / \"bench/programs/coremark.elf\")\n    # 50M cycle ceiling: 2K CoreMark with ITERATIONS=10 + iStall+dStall\n    # needs ~5M cycles. 50M gives 10x headroom for slower candidates.\n    try:\n        result = run_pgroup(\n            [sim_bin, elf, \"50000000\"] + COREMARK_SIM_FLAGS,\n            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=600, env=env\n        )\n    except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError) as e:\n        return {'completed': False, 'iter_per_cycle': 0.0, 'cycles': 0, 'iterations': 0,\n                'reason': f'coremark_harness_error: {e}'}\n    lines = [l for l in result.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()]\n    if len(lines) < 2:\n        return {'completed': False, 'iter_per_cycle': 0.0, 'cycles': 0, 'iterations': 0,\n                'reason': 'no output from sim'}\n\n    # Last line is the ebreak/maxcycles marker; second-to-last is the final retirement.\n    try:\n        marker = json.loads(lines[-1])\n        last_retirement = json.loads(lines[-2])\n    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n        return {'completed': False, 'iter_per_cycle': 0.0, 'cycles': 0, 'iterations': 0,\n                'reason': f'malformed sim output: {e}'}\n\n    if not marker.get('ebreak', False):\n        # Benchmark ran out of cycles before finishing. Score 0 — don't credit\n        # a hung CPU with whatever cycle count it happened to reach.\n        return {'completed': False, 'iter_per_cycle': 0.0, 'cycles': 0, 'iter\n…[6688 of 9760 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c032288e54273ef1]…\nnch_failed': True, 'placement_failed': False,\n                'reason': 'fpga_report_unparsed: no Logic/Register count in Gowin report',\n                'fmax_mhz': fp['fmax_mhz'], 'seeds': fp['seeds']}\n\n    cm = run_coremark_ipc(worktree, sim_bin, env=env)\n    if not cm['completed']:\n        return {\n            'bench_failed': True,\n            'reason': cm.get('reason', 'unknown'),\n            'fmax_mhz': fp['fmax_mhz'],\n            'seeds': fp['seeds'],\n            'placement_failed': False,\n        }\n\n    fitness = fp['fmax_mhz'] * cm['iter_per_cycle'] * 1_000_000  # iter/sec\n    return {\n        **fp,\n        'ipc_coremark':  round(cm['iter_per_cycle'], 6),\n        'fitness':       round(fitness, 2),\n        'cycles':        cm['cycles'],\n        'iterations':    cm['iterations'],\n    }\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    import sys\n    result = run_fpga_eval(\n        sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else '.',\n        sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else None,\n    )\n    print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
