'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,time\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all\nwt=Path.cwd(); root=wt/'cores/bench/generated/hyp-r11'\nfor name in ['narrow_registered','rf_bank4','rf_bank16']:\n r=build_all(wt,root/name/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',root/name/'timing',options=(1,))\n (root/name/'results.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\n print(name,json.dumps(r),flush=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_37", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r11s0.yaml')\nh={'id':'hyp-20261004-001-r11s0','title':'Complete narrow MUL directly from registered operands in one DSP stage','category':'micro_opt','motivation':'''The champion scores 238.34 iterations/sec at 107.897 MHz. CoreMark\\\\ncontains many products of sign-extended small integers, while the shared\\\\nmultiplier always computes four 16x16 partial products and a separate\\\\nreduction for low MUL. The earlier single-cycle general MUL regressed\\\\nFmax. New exploratory request-edge signed-17-bit MUL trials retain\\\\ncanonical CRCs and reduce bracketed cycles to 4,338,144, but their\\\\nunregistered forwarded-operand-to-DSP path loses too much clock speed.\\\\nLES SONS.md is absent; the micro_opt history has not tried completing\\\\nnarrow products directly from the existing registered operands.\\\\n'''.replace('LES SONS','LESSONS'), 'hypothesis':'''Keep the multiplier request snapshot and introduce one operand-width\\\\nfast path only for ALU_MUL. At acceptance capture a narrow-fit bit:\\\\na[31:16] equals sixteen copies of a[16], and likewise for b. These\\\\nconditions exactly recognize signed integers in [-65536,65535],\\\\nincluding every zero-extended 16-bit value. Compute the signed 17x17\\\\nproduct from a_q[16:0] and b_q[16:0], explicitly retain its full 34-bit\\\\nmathematical width and use only bits [31:0]. In PRODUCTS, when the saved\\\\nop is MUL and the saved fit bit is true, capture that exact product\\\\nin the existing result register and go directly to DONE, skipping\\\\nREDUCE. All wider MUL and every high-half multiply keep their original\\\\npartial-product, carry and signed-correction states. Register the fit\\\\nbit with the accepted operands and opcode; never use live request\\\\noperands, live opcode or live width qualification during ownership.\\\\n\\\\nThis deliberately keeps the existing operand register before the DSP:\\\\nno direct forwarded-EX-to-product capture, new CPU stage, multiplier\\\\ncache, predictor change or ALU transformation is part of the proposal.\\\\nThe local DSP is allowed to share hardware with the existing products\\\\nwhen synthesis can do so without putting a source mux on a critical\\\\npath. Ensure constant operand slicing and signed casts give a genuine\\\\n17x17 multiplication, not sign extension into another 32x32 multiplier.\\\\nThe width predicate must be exact for negatives and boundary values.\\\\nReset clears the fit flag; reset still cancels requests and results.\\\\n\\\\nKeep valid/ready acceptance and consumption, stable held results,\\\\nexclusive M ownership, saved source/RVFI snapshots, launch/busy frontend\\\\nholds, older-memory priority and exactly-once transfer unchanged.\\\\nThe accepted narrow MUL now has one calculation edge after request\\\\nacceptance, instead of two; consumption remains a separate edge.\\\\nNo other instruction changes latency. Preserve all real arithmetic\\\\nand all ALTOPS substitutions, default-illegal decode, alignment traps,\\\\nnret=1 ports, full architectural RVFI and consecutive retirement.\\\\n\\\\nCompare scored median Gowin timing times actual marker-bracketed cycles.\\\\nRequire all fixed formal/cosim gates, canonical CoreMark CRCs and\\\\nvalidation literal, bounded accesses and exact start/stop timing.\\\\nUpdate latency-specific unit expectations only to independently derived\\\\noperand-dependent latency, preserving all bit-result and handshake\\\\nassertions and testing both fast and fallback paths. The gain depends\\\\non how many products qualify and whether the saved-operand DSP maps\\\\nwithout adding result-selection delay; confidence is low until measured.\\\\n''','expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},'changes':[{'file':'rtl/multiplier.sv','description':'''Register exact signed-17-bit fit with accepted operands. Complete\\\\nqualifying low MUL directly in PRODUCTS using a signed 17x17 product\\\\nof the retained operands. Keep full-width fallback, high-half\\\\ncorrections, reset, request/result transfers and held output intact.\\\\n'''},{'file':'test/test_multiplier.py','description':'''Retain every arithmetic, busy rejection, backpressure, reset and\\\\ntransfer check. Derive expected fast-path membership independently\\\\nfrom signed Python integer bounds. Check one versus two calculation\\\\nedges for narrow versus wide low MUL, unchanged high-MUL latency,\\\\nall sign combinations, -65537/-65536/-65535 and 65534/65535/65536\\\\nboundaries, alternating narrow/wide requests, random products, changed\\\\nlive inputs after acceptance and reset during both paths.\\\\n'''},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all architectural assertions and adjust only internal\\\\nMUL-latency assumptions to the documented operand-dependent service.\\\\nCover narrow and wide MUL dependencies, x0, mixed M instructions,\\\\nstores/branches using results, both bus stalls, retained WB operands,\\\\nbackpressured completion and reset before each possible transfer.\\\\nVerify full RVFI bit results, source snapshots and exact retirement.\\\\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=78))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('VALID revised candidate',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
