{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_30", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r5s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261004-001-r5s0\ntitle: \\\"Explicit banked register file with local flops and shallow read muxes\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 140.75 iterations/sec at 67.190 MHz and\n  4,773,836 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent.\n  Prior micro_opt attempts targeted obsolete combinational division,\n  redirect feedback, operand mux folding and single-cycle low MUL.\n  The latest accepted change registered complete ALU operand selections,\n  while the low-MUL fast path regressed Fmax to 51.019 MHz. A fresh Gowin\n  run reproduces placement-option-0 Fmax of 65.364 MHz. Its worst path\n  starts at a register-file block RAM output, crosses ID read selection,\n  EX operand selection and integer ALU logic, and ends at a data-memory\n  address input. The register-file RAM contributes 2.260 ns clock-to-output.\n  Explicitly changing this small storage array's implementation can remove\n  that launch point and retain a fast, separate ID/EX operand boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only reg_file.sv's generic indexed storage and read expressions\n  with an explicit 31-word flop implementation and two combinational,\n  hierarchical read networks. Generate a separately named 32-bit register\n  for each architectural register x1 through x31, each with its own\n  constant-address write-enable comparison and the existing synchronous\n  reset-to-zero behavior. Provide x0 as a constant zero word. Do not\n  describe the storage as an array written through a variable index;\n  expose constant-index word wires only for assembling the read network.\n\n  Partition each read port into four eight-word groups. Within each group,\n  compare the low three read-address bits with the eight constant word\n  positions and OR the eight masked 32-bit words. Select the resulting\n  group using read-address bits [4:3]. Preserve the existing final\n  same-cycle WB write-first bypass, with the x0-zero case taking priority,\n  and preserve a single synchronous write port. Both read ports must\n  produce the identical architectural values as before for every address,\n  write, reset and stall sequence. The four groups are mux partitions;\n  they introduce no access restriction or bank-conflict stalls.\n\n  This is one FPGA mapping micro-optimization: replace slow inferred\n  register-file block RAM with local flops and bounded read selection,\n  preserving pipeline stages, instruction latency, forwarding, decoded\n  controls, ALU arithmetic, multiply/divide ownership, bus handshakes,\n  nret=1 IO and retirement order. The intended gain is higher Fmax at\n  exactly the same bracketed CoreMark cycle count. A private trial of\n  this structure measures 72.829 MHz for Gowin placement option 0,\n  versus the reproduced parent's 65.364 MHz; the worst path now starts\n  at the WB result selection. It uses 4,249 LUTs and 1,759 registers,\n  with four block RAMs, zero LUT RAMs and two DSPs, versus the parent's\n  3,057 LUTs, 818 registers, six block RAMs and two DSPs. All five\n  unchanged register-file cocotb tests pass. Placement medians, full\n  sequential equivalence and stalled CoreMark validation remain to be\n  checked. The main risk is routing pressure from additional flops and\n  read muxes; evaluate the vendor median, all unchanged correctness\n  gates and canonical CoreMark CRCs before acceptance. At unchanged\n  cycles, clearing the 4.6 percent margin requires roughly 70.281 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement x1-x31 as separately generated synchronous-reset flops\n      with constant-address write enables and x0 as constant zero.\n      Build each asynchronous read as four eight-word masked-OR mux\n      groups selected by address[4:3]. Retain the exact module ports,\n      single-write behavior, reset values and x0-prioritized WB bypass.\n      Keep the existing ID/EX register boundary and prevent generic\n      variable-index storage from recreating the block-RAM read path.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all existing tests. Extend coverage to every read-address\n      pair, writes and WB bypass collisions on both ports across all\n      four mux groups, x0 writes and reads, consecutive writes, and\n      reset after nonzero writes to every register. Include seeded\n      random interleaved reads and writes against an independent\n      architectural register-array model. Require unchanged pipeline\n      tests, formal checks and stalled RVFI cosim/CoreMark gates during\n      implementation, plus scored median Gowin timing and unchanged\n      marker-bracketed cycles.\n''')\nprint('Updated hypothesis to banked flop register file')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
