l a register\nresult. Only misaligned JAL/JALR can cancel an EX-stage writer. This\nremoves the conditional-branch comparison from both actual EX/MEM\nwrite controls and next_reg_write feeding retimed bypass selection.\nPreserve the existing completed-M snapshot eligibility and MEM's\npost-load-alignment suppression. Every trapping instruction must still\nretire with its original trap semantics and with zero register write\nand memory masks; no decoder or trap check may be weakened.\n\nCapture effective addresses on precisely the same EX/MEM transfer edge\nas the associated instruction and memory controls. Hold the entire\nrequest addre\n…[17248 of 20320 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1a4f282a14145426]…\ncursor without early shift\nmasking reached only 107.294 MHz median, supporting the chosen topology.\n\nThe complete trial passes warning-free Verilator lint in both real and\nALTOPS modes, all 53 unchanged single-channel ALTOPS formal checks, and\nall eight selftest/random full-RVFI cosim comparisons with and without\nboth bus stalls. Canonical stalled CoreMark retains all five required\nCRCs, validation literal and bounded accesses. Its start/stop markers\nare 15,648 and 4,542,719: exactly 4,527,071 cycles for ten iterations,\nidentical to the champion. Median timing times this throughput implies\nabout 240.46 iterations/sec, an 8.0 percent gain over 222.67. Predict\neight percent from this evidence. These are private trial results;\nimplementation must reproduce scored timing/cycles and all fixed gates.\nThe main risks are selector fanout, synthesis factoring and placement\nof the combined OR network. Acceptance requires fitness above 232.91282\niterations/sec and median Gowin Fmax above 105.443076 MHz at these cycles.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
