{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_49", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r10s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261004-002-r10s1\ntitle: \\\"Use synchronous mirrored block-RAM register reads at the ID/EX boundary\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 238.34 iterations/sec at 107.897 MHz with 4,527,071\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its resettable 31-by-32 register storage and\n  two combinational read trees occupy many flops, LUTs and routes. The\n  current ID/EX operand flops already establish a synchronous operand\n  boundary. Native FPGA synchronous RAM reads can implement that same\n  boundary while removing the wide resettable storage and decode read mux\n  network. LESSONS.md is absent. Prior fetch buffering and extra recovery\n  stages lost fitness to timing or cycle costs. Private forwarding-\n  qualification and JALR-interlock trials also failed vendor timing, so\n  neither is included. A storage/topology change offers greater structural\n  leverage than moving another Boolean qualifier between existing muxes.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the core's flop-based combinational register-file reads with two\n  mirrored 32-by-32 memories, each with one synchronous write port and one\n  synchronous read port. Both copies receive every architectural nonzero\n  register write; one supplies rs1 and the other rs2. Describe unreset data\n  arrays using Gowin-supported synchronous RAM inference templates, with\n  explicit clock enables and no vendor black boxes in the formal model.\n  Target two block-RAM read banks rather than distributed LUT mux trees;\n  confirm actual inferred resources in the vendor report.\n\n  Read addresses are the current raw decode source fields. On precisely\n  the existing ID/EX payload capture edge, enable both synchronous reads\n  and capture their zero/initialization and same-edge-WB conflict metadata.\n  Feed the resulting registered RAM operands directly into EX's fallback\n  operand inputs instead of recapturing them in ID/EX a cycle late. These\n  RAM read outputs are the existing operand register boundary: instruction,\n  PC, immediate, decoder controls, prediction metadata and bypass choices\n  still capture on that SAME edge. This adds neither an instruction stage\n  nor a dependency bubble. Hold read outputs and all associated metadata\n  whenever ID/EX payload holds for older dmem backpressure or M ownership.\n  Invalid payload captures may read normally, preserving payload/valid\n  separation. Keep the existing prioritized registered EX/MEM and WB\n  forwarding and complete parallel ALU choices, replacing only their\n  unforwarded regfile source. Every branch, store, M-request snapshot and\n  RVFI operand must use those same correct post-forward values.\n\n  Preserve architectural reset-to-zero without clearing the RAM data\n  arrays: reset one valid bit per architectural word, and capture a\n  per-read invalid/x0 indication with the read address. A word whose valid\n  bit is clear supplies zero. Nonzero writes set its valid bit. Reset\n  clears validity and all read/conflict metadata, so stale or uninitialized\n  RAM words never escape after cold or warm reset. x0 always reads zero\n  and is never written. A same-edge architectural WB write to a read\n  address must override the RAM's potentially unspecified read-during-\n  write result: register each conflict match and one shared WB data word\n  on the consumer's read edge, then select that captured data in EX.\n  The conflict override has priority over the old invalid bit, ensuring\n  a register's first write is immediately visible. Hold the captured\n  conflict data and flags with the RAM outputs during backend stalls.\n  Do not use live WB data or live read addresses to resolve an older read.\n\n  Integrate this synchronous interface explicitly into core/ID/EX. Remove\n  functionally redundant ID/EX copies of regfile data if appropriate,\n  while retaining full architectural RVFI source snapshots from EX. A\n  parameterized asynchronous compatibility implementation may preserve\n  existing standalone reg_file tests; the actual core must elaborate the\n  synchronous mirrored implementation. Avoid a combinational output mux\n  between synchronous and asynchronous modes. If the inferred RAM's\n  output clock enable cannot implement the required hold directly, use\n  its supported output register/enable arrangement rather than advancing\n  operands independently of their instruction.\n\n  Keep pipeline transfer timing, load-use bubbles, registered recovery,\n  direction prediction/training, exact integer and RV32M results/ALTOPS,\n  M ownership and completion, actual writer eligibility, retained WB\n  forwarding, request stability, decoder default-illegal discipline,\n  alignment traps, nret=1 memory/RVFI IO and consecutive retirement intact.\n  This is one structural storage/read-boundary replacement, with no cache,\n  predictor change, speculative fault handling or extra pipeline stage.\n  The expected gain comes from fewer mux/storage routes and shorter decode\n  read paths at unchanged measured cycles. The added registered WB-conflict\n  selection, block-RAM clock-to-output delay and EX arithmetic may become\n  limiting paths. A resource report that still implements the core's two\n  read banks as the original flop/mux network does not establish the\n  proposed mechanism.\n\n  Validate unchanged formal and full-RVFI cosim gates and canonical stalled\n  CoreMark CRCs, validation literal, bounds and marker timing. Unit tests\n  must retain architectural expectations while adapting only their former\n  assumptions that regfile outputs always describe the live decode bus.\n  Add independent checks for consumer/operand alignment, both simultaneous\n  read conflicts, first writes after reset, x0, cold/warm reset with stale\n  RAM contents, held reads while WB advances, load/M dependencies, older\n  dmem holds, imem stalls, wrong-path captures and reset during a hold.\n  Compare actual sources/results/traps/memory effects and RVFI order with\n  an architectural reference. Measure all scored Gowin placement options\n  and require unchanged 4,527,071 marker-bracketed cycles. Acceptance needs\n  fitness above 249.30364 iterations/sec, equivalent to median Fmax above\n  112.861528 MHz at those cycles. Predict ten percent with low confidence\n  until vendor inference and timing demonstrate the storage benefit.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement two mirrored synchronous-read/write RAM banks for the core,\n      with payload read enable, resettable per-word validity, registered\n      x0/uninitialized masks and exact registered WB conflict bypass.\n      Preserve same-edge first-write visibility, hold all read metadata\n      and data together, and keep an optional standalone asynchronous mode.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Elaborate the synchronous register-file mode, drive its read capture\n      enable from the existing ID/EX payload advance, and connect registered\n      read operands directly to EX fallback inputs. Preserve all forwarding,\n      hazards, transfer cycles, memory/RVFI ports and retirement connections.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep instruction/control capture on the same edge as synchronous RF\n      reads. Eliminate any extra capture of already registered RF operands\n      into a later operand boundary; retain raw read addresses and the exact\n      decoded-payload/valid hold and kill protocol.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the synchronous RF output operands as the unforwarded choices in\n      ordinary and complete ALU forwarding muxes. Snapshot their resolved\n      architectural values for RVFI, stores, branches and M acceptance.\n      Keep producer eligibility, arithmetic/recovery and memory lanes intact.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Remove redundant ID/EX regfile-value fields if their storage is moved\n      into the RAM read boundary. Preserve all consumer identity/control\n      metadata and post-forward EX/MEM and MEM/WB RVFI source snapshots.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing asynchronous architectural checks and add tests of the\n      actual synchronous mode: two read ports, same-edge WB collisions,\n      first writes, x0, enabled/held reads and reset-to-zero without array\n      reset. Model reads from pre-edge architectural state plus explicit\n      same-edge write-first override, independent of the DUT RAM mode.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve every architectural check while adapting only internal\n      assumptions about live decode versus registered RF outputs. Verify\n      exact consumer/operand association, forwarding and held reads under\n      both bus stalls, M ownership, load-use, recovery and warm reset.\n''')\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Final storage hypothesis written and schema-valid')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
