{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_55", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r12s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Make JALR recovery unconditional and qualify its write after registration\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 238.34 iterations/sec at 107.897 MHz and 4,527,071\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path runs from the registered\n  arithmetic result through forwarded JALR alignment into the next forwarding\n  selection. Parallel alignment, registered source-specific alignment and\n  synchronous RAM operands all regressed. LESSONS.md is absent. Remove this\n  feedback structurally by giving every validated JALR a registered recovery\n  event, and cancel its architectural write using a registered fault in MEM.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the existing single-issue pipeline, instruction schedules for aligned\n  instructions, predictor and nret=1 interface. Every valid, decoded-legal\n  JALR entering EX/MEM schedules the existing registered recovery, regardless\n  of target alignment. An aligned JALR recovers to its exact forwarded\n  rs1-plus-immediate target with bit zero cleared. A misaligned JALR retires\n  with the existing trap, no destination write and sequential pc_next, and\n  recovers to its own PC+4 to refetch the younger sequential instruction.\n  Thus both outcomes kill the resident younger ID/EX instruction and the\n  incoming fetch on the following recovery cycle before either has effects.\n\n  Separate anticipated EX/MEM bypass eligibility from architectural commit\n  eligibility for this instruction class. In EX advertise a valid decoded\n  JALR writer without consulting its forwarded target alignment, and capture\n  its PC+4 result normally. Capture the exact JALR alignment fault as a\n  separate scalar alongside EX/MEM; retain exact trap and RVFI pc_next.\n  In MEM cancel reg_write with that registered scalar before next_reg_write,\n  MEM/WB capture, regfile write or retirement. Direct JAL, branch and memory\n  faults retain their existing exact qualification. The tentative JALR\n  bypass can only reach a younger consumer that unconditional recovery\n  squashes be\n…[2518 of 5590 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:cc4f6346517b73c9]…\nile: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Schedule registered recovery for every valid legal JALR independently\n      of target alignment. Advertise and capture its tentative link writer\n      without live alignment qualification, register its exact alignment\n      fault, and select recovery from existing registered effective address\n      or PC+4 arithmetic lanes. Retain actual trap and sequential fault PC,\n      existing holds, younger squash and M ownership discipline.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Cancel a faulting JALR's architectural reg_write using its registered\n      fault before MEM/WB capture and upcoming-WB bypass eligibility. Keep\n      memory alignment, requests, load data and retained forwarding intact.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all current checks and add exact RVFI and memory-effect tests\n      for unconditional JALR recovery, misalignment cancellation and\n      sequential refetch across dependencies, bypass ages and bus holds.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
