{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_75", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r12s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Decouple fetch with a three-entry FIFO and local one-hot occupancy control\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 238.34 iterations/sec at 107.897 MHz with 4,527,071\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. The earlier two-entry fetch queue reduced that\n  run to 4,190,068 cycles but reached only 99.018 MHz, narrowly losing overall.\n  LESSONS.md is absent. A six-entry precursor reduced cycles to 4,157,542,\n  showing little benefit from that much storage, while its binary occupancy,\n  subtraction-based append selection and backend-to-payload control paths\n  limited timing to 79.618 MHz. The most promising refinement is a small\n  queue whose occupancy and payload controls are local, rather than simply\n  increasing depth or changing the register file.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one three-entry fall-through instruction FIFO between the existing\n  speculative fetch PC/predictor and decode. Keep single issue, pipeline\n  depth for directly delivered instructions, the existing direction predictor,\n  all arithmetic and nret=1. Fetch may advance during load-use, dmem and M\n  ownership holds until FIFO capacity is exhausted. Decode consumes a queued\n  head even when the instruction bus is stalled. Empty-queue direct delivery\n  retains the champion's zero extra stage latency.\n\n  Implement occupancy as four one-hot states for zero through three entries.\n  Empty and full are direct registered bits. Each entry owns a constant-index\n  payload register and a local append enable; the head is a fixed register,\n  not a variable-index RAM or pointer-selected read. Store only the 98 payload\n  bits: PC, instruction, prepared recovery address, saved predicted_taken and\n  direct_aligned. Availability comes from occupancy or a ready direct fetch,\n  independently of stored validity. Use explicit packed-vector payload\n  registers inside generate scopes, with constant-index wires between them,\n  to preserve identical elaboration in Gowin, Verilator and Yosys. The\n  struct-array precursor passed concrete sim\n…[4339 of 7411 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:30dee1eb56835ef5]…\nqueue capacity to fetch hold, queue-head availability to decode, and\n      existing backend/load-use backpressure to consumption. Preserve\n      predictor training, all execution/retirement wiring and top-level IO.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep speculative target preparation and training unchanged. Treat\n      stall as producer capacity/bus hold, and expose raw fetch metadata\n      independently of decode availability. Recovery retains PC priority.\n  - file: test/test_fetch_queue.py\n    description: |\n      Compare accepted and delivered complete payloads against an independent\n      Python FIFO across every occupancy and handshake combination, empty\n      bypass, full replacement, random stalls, flush and reset.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Include the new module, model accepted fetches and buffered decode\n      independently, preserve every architectural/RVFI assertion and check\n      queue conservation and recovery squash under hazards and both stalls.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
