{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r3s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-002-r3s1\ntitle: \"Isolate multiplication in a registered shared DSP execution unit\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 119.64 at 53.739 MHz and 4,491,817 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. The previous structural changes removed unrolled\n  division and redirect-to-decode feedback; the latest worst Gowin path\n  now crosses register-file/forwarding, EX and the combinational ALU\n  multiplier. The production ALU still describes three full-width\n  products with signed, unsigned and mixed signedness, all feeding the\n  ordinary result mux. Their delay constrains every instruction even\n  though multiplication is only a subset of CoreMark's dynamic stream.\n  The structural history contains no registered-multiplier attempt and\n  LESSONS.md is absent.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move MUL, MULH, MULHU and MULHSU out of the single-cycle integer ALU\n  into one clocked, shared multiply execution unit. Keep single issue,\n  in-order completion and the current nret=1 interface. Snapshot both\n  forwarded operands and the operation when EX accepts a valid multiply,\n  using a register boundary before the DSP logic. In the next cycle\n  compute and register four unsigned 16x16 partial products, denoted\n  ll, lh, hl and hh for the low/high operand halves. The reduction cycle\n  computes an 18-bit column = ll[31:16] + lh[15:0] + hl[15:0], with all\n  terms explicitly zero extended. MUL's result is\n  {column[15:0], ll[15:0]} and is marked complete immediately after this\n  reduction edge. In parallel, register high_base = hh + lh[31:16] +\n  hl[31:16] modulo 2^32 and the two-bit column[17:16] carry. A subsequent\n  high-half cycle registers high_base + carry, completing MULHU. Avoid a\n  combinational path\n  through operand forwarding, a full 32x32 multiplier, product reduction\n  and the main ALU result mux in a single clock period.\n\n  Share the unsigned product across all four operations. MUL returns\n  its low 32 bits, MULHU its high 32 bits; MULHSU subtracts b from the\n  high half when a is negative, and MULH additionally \n…[5582 of 8654 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1536ed931a6be8cb]…\nner source list. Keep integer\n      operations combinational and divider tests unchanged.\n  - file: test/test_multiplier.py\n    description: |\n      Test all four actual multiply operations against exact Python\n      arithmetic, including randomized operands, signed extrema,\n      cross-product carries, zero/x0-like values, result backpressure,\n      consecutive requests and reset during execution. Check bounded\n      completion, stable held results and exactly-once transfer.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add integration coverage for multiply dependencies into ALU,\n      branch, JALR and stores; x0 destinations; consecutive multiply and\n      divide operations; older stalled loads/stores; instruction stalls;\n      completion under dmem backpressure; and reset during ownership.\n      Verify forwarded source snapshots, request stability, no wrong-\n      path effects, single retirement and exact RVFI order. Include\n      multiplier.sv in the explicit source list and retain all checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
