{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_18", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r4s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-002-r4s1\ntitle: \"Separate a registered load/store request path from integer writeback\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 129.85 iterations/sec at 61.988 MHz and\n  4,773,836 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior structural changes removed\n  combinational division, redirect feedback into decode, and multiplication\n  from the ordinary EX result path. The latest worst Gowin path is still\n  21.653 ns through operand selection and the integer ALU to data-memory\n  addressing. The current EX/MEM alu_result serves both integer writeback\n  and every data-bus address, including non-memory instructions, while\n  MEM performs store formatting, byte enables and alignment gating after\n  that register. LESSONS.md is absent; no recorded structural attempt has\n  separated the memory-request datapath from the general ALU result.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give load/store execution a dedicated address generator and registered\n  request bundle at the existing EX/MEM boundary, without adding a stage.\n  Compute effective_address = forwarded_rs1 + decoded_immediate directly,\n  independently of the integer ALU's operand and result selectors, JAL\n  link mux and M-extension result mux. Keep the integer result register\n  and its existing forwarding paths for ordinary register-producing\n  instructions. Loads are forwarded only after actual load completion.\n\n  Register the exact byte address, replicated store data, four-bit byte\n  mask, read/write request bits, width, signedness and alignment-fault\n  metadata together. Format SB/SH/SW data and derive lane masks in EX;\n  check LH/LHU/SH and LW/SW alignment from the dedicated effective address\n  before the request-register edge. A valid aligned LOAD/STORE produces\n  one request. Invalid payloads, illegal instructions, M-operation bubbles\n  and misaligned accesses produce no read or write enable. Suppress the\n  destination write on an alignment fault and retire the same trap and\n  RVFI values as the current implementation.\n\n  MEM drives memory-side outputs directly\n…[2454 of 5526 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:33ee00e65904e281]…\nry. Retain request payload when no\n      new memory operation advances; clear enables on bubbles and\n      non-memory advancement, and hold everything on dmem stalls.\n      Preserve M-unit ownership, snapshots and integer forwarding.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive dmem address, write data and enables from the dedicated\n      registered request. Use its address and alignment metadata for\n      load extraction, traps and aligned RVFI memory fields. Preserve\n      original bus acceptance, MEM/WB latency and retained WB bypass.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing assertions and add dependent load/store address\n      chains, all legal byte/half/word lane cases, alignment traps,\n      non-memory instructions between requests, prolonged data stalls,\n      instruction stalls and M-operation bubbles. Check stable held\n      requests, exactly-once stores and retirement, forwarded store\n      operands, memory masks, loaded values and no wrong-path effects.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
