{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_37", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r4s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-002-r4s1\ntitle: \"Flop-backed banked register file preserves the operand pipeline boundary\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 129.85 iterations/sec at 61.988 MHz and 4,773,836\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. Previous structural\n  changes removed unrolled division, redirect feedback and combinational\n  multiplication; the latest path remains register file through forwarding\n  and the integer ALU. A local unmodified Gowin build reproduces option-0\n  Fmax 61.131 MHz and the 21.653 ns reported worst path. Its detailed\n  report identifies the register file as BSRAM, with 2.260 ns clock-to-Q,\n  followed by operand selection, forwarding and ALU logic into the data\n  RAM address. Gowin absorbs the ID/EX operand read registers into the\n  synchronous RAM ports, leaving no discrete operand flop on this path.\n  No previous structural hypothesis has changed register-file storage.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the inferred indexed 32x32 register-file memory with 31 explicit\n  32-bit flop-backed words and a constant x0. Give each word its own local\n  write enable decoded from w_en and w_addr. Keep synchronous reset to\n  zero for every stored word. Do not describe a dynamically indexed\n  read/write memory array or rely on an unverified vendor RAM attribute:\n  use generated scalar word registers so the storage is ordinary flops.\n\n  Implement both asynchronous read ports as one-hot masked reductions:\n  for each nonzero word, AND its value with the replicated equality of\n  that port's five-bit address and the constant word index. OR eight\n  words per bank and then OR the four banks. Balanced bank reductions\n  limit mux depth and keep the physical read topology explicit. Keep\n  the existing write-first bypass on both ports and x0 priority over\n  bypass. A write to x0 changes no state. Same-address dual reads and\n  same-cycle writes retain exactly the current observable behavior.\n\n  Preserve the existing ID/EX operand registers, their reset, backend\n  hold enables and payload/valid \n…[2012 of 5084 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7a99b18882bc38f9]…\ns in\n      four banks of eight words. Preserve the module interface, hardwired\n      x0, both read ports, same-cycle write-first bypass and all reset\n      semantics. Confirm Gowin maps storage to flops and retains the\n      existing ID/EX operand register boundary.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every existing test. Add distinct patterns in all registers,\n      cross-bank and same-address dual reads, adjacent writes, writes to\n      x0, write-first bypass on both ports, and reset after nonzero state.\n      Check every word retains its value when another bank is written.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all pipeline assertions and add dependencies across all\n      register-file banks, both WB-to-ID read collisions, dual-source\n      same-register reads and reset after writes. Cover instruction/data\n      stalls, load-use dependencies, M-operation ownership and dependent\n      stores/branches, verifying exact source values and retirement order.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
