{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r7s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261004-002-r7s1',\n 'title':'Partition EX into independently registered memory and arithmetic result lanes',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The 181.41 iterations/sec champion reaches 86.601 MHz with 4,773,836 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path starts at ID/EX alu_op and passes through M-unit selection into EX and the data-memory endpoint. The architectural-result register is also the bus-address source, and its whole-bundle M busy/completion mux couples request payloads to irrelevant arithmetic state. LESSONS.md is absent. The earlier r5s1 address partition was measured before the banked register file and registered WB result became the champion. Repeating its shared bundle mux on today's champion reaches only 83.104 MHz. Independently capturing the address and store-data fields instead reaches 89.333 MHz at unchanged instruction latency, making explicit resource ownership the promising structural angle.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Partition the existing EX/MEM boundary by destination resource, retaining pipeline depth, single issue and nret=1. Create a dedicated registered effective address from the actual forwarded architectural rs1 plus immediate. MEM uses this register exclusively for the external address, alignment, subword extraction, byte masks and aligned RVFI memory address. The arithmetic/link result remains the early register-result bypass source and WB source for nonloads.\n\nThe memory request address and raw store-data registers capture on every edge without an outstanding older dmem hold, independently of M launch, busy, result-valid, saved M metadata and whole-bundle bubble insertion. Store data captures actual forwarded rs2. An inert instruction may change these payloads but must never issue a request. During an outstanding memory hold retain the complete address, data, width and request strobes. Completed M instructions must not substitute their saved arithmetic result or snapshot into the request-payload registers. This independent capture is essential: merely adding an address field inside the existing zero/snapshot/normal mux did not improve the current champion.\n\nComplete the resource partition by giving the clocked M service a separate result path into the saved M instruction's EX/MEM arithmetic result. Normal integer and link results never select registered multiply/divide data through the encoded integer ALU result case. Register validated multiply/divide class bits with ID/EX; use them for dispatch and wait control. Keep operation subtype for real arithmetic and ALTOPS. On acceptance remember the selected unit. With one outstanding operation, completion comes from the exclusive units' combined result-valid, qualified by EX's registered active ownership, and result data is selected by saved unit ownership. Result consumption uses this owner, not a live opcode. A compatibility parameter may retain the existing standalone ALU test interface; the core must elaborate the separated topology.\n\nKeep arithmetic and transfer latency exactly unchanged, including low MUL reduction, signed high-half corrections and iterative division. Snapshot actual forwarded operands and RVFI metadata once on request acceptance. Hold ID/EX, drain older instructions, insert inert bubbles, retain stable completed results under backpressure, transfer exactly once, and release the frontend only on consumption. Keep existing forwarding priority, x0, retained retired WB data during holds, load-use and redirect timing, decoder default-illegal behavior, reset and channel-0 monotonic order. Preserve exact single-cycle ALTOPS formulas and all real arithmetic.\n\nPreserve aggregate branch/jump alignment fault for trapping, redirect suppression and sequential trapped next PC. Qualify register-write producer eligibility by jump alignment fault alone: legal conditional branches never write rd, so their operand comparator need not feed a register-write enable. Memory alignment traps still cancel load writes and all external strobes. This is class-local side-effect qualification within the same resource partition, not a weakened trap check.\n\nVerify all unchanged unit suites, fixed formal and cosim gates, canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs, validation literal, bounded accesses and marker timing. Compare scored median Gowin Fmax and require removal of the generic ALU/M completion mux from memory request payloads. At unchanged cycles acceptance requires fitness above 189.75486 iterations/sec, approximately 90.586 MHz. The independent request-register trial reaches 89.333 MHz (3.15 percent), insufficient alone; the complete arithmetic/request partition must clear the threshold. Further routing or branch bottlenecks are the main risk. Do not combine this with a predictor, cache, faster multiplier arithmetic or another instruction stage.''',\n 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add a dedicated EX/MEM effective-address field and registered validated M execution-class bits in ID/EX. Preserve all architectural and RVFI metadata, original result forwarding fields and nret=1.'},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Capture validated multiply/divide classes with the decoded consumer using identical payload holds, reset and independent validity kills. Keep original operation subtype, decoder legality and source addressing.'},\n {'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'Expose separate normal integer and owned M result paths; remove real M data from the integer result mux in core mode. Use predecoded classes for unit dispatch, remember the accepted unit, combine exclusive completion indications and consume by saved ownership. Preserve arithmetic, ALTOPS and standalone ALU test semantics.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Capture effective address and forwarded store data independently of the whole-bundle M busy/completion mux whenever dmem is not holding an outstanding request. Route owned M results directly to saved arithmetic-result metadata. Preserve request/consumption edges, bubbles and snapshots. Keep aggregate traps exact while isolating writer eligibility from non-writing conditional-branch comparison.'},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Use the dedicated effective address for every memory-side address, alignment, lane and load-extraction operation, while retaining the ordinary arithmetic result for nonload WB and early forwarding. Preserve masks, trap suppression, stable held requests and raw RVFI memory data.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain every existing assertion. Add independent request-payload coverage across M launch/busy/completion, dependent address bases and store values, load/M-produced rs1/rs2, all lanes, alignment faults, x0, redirects, reset and simultaneous bus stalls. Compare exact RVFI sources/results, memory effects, single retirement, strict order and outstanding request stability.'},\n {'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'Retain every arithmetic vector and standalone selection/handshake assertion. Exercise separated service outputs, accepted-unit ownership, stable held M results, alternating multiply/divide operations and unchanged completion latency without weakening real arithmetic or ALTOPS checks.'}\n ]}\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\nprint('Refined hypothesis written and schema validated:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
