{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r9s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261004-002-r9s1',\n'title':'Decouple instruction fetch from backend holds with a two-entry elastic queue',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion reaches 222.67 iterations/sec at 100.806 MHz and 4,527,071 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its combinational IF/ID topology couples PC advancement to load-use bubbles, data-memory backpressure and owned M execution. Fetch therefore wastes instruction-ready cycles while the backend holds, and decode later bubbles whenever the instruction bus is unavailable. With independent approximately 22-percent stalls on each bus, buffering useful fetches during backend holds can reduce this compounded loss. LESSONS.md is absent. The structural log shows successful resource and control isolation but a regression from an extra recovery cycle without latency hiding. Reversible integer-lane trials in this session reached only 93.545, 90.407, 96.073 and 99.866 MHz, so the final proposal targets useful instruction delivery rather than those unsuccessful result-selection changes.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Insert one two-entry elastic instruction queue between the existing speculative fetch PC/predictor and decode, preserving single issue, in-order execution and nret=1. The fetch PC advances only when a real io_imemReady instruction is enqueued and space is available. Backend load-use, dmem and M holds inhibit queue consumption, not fetch production. Continue prefetching along the existing predicted path until the queue fills. No instruction or data cache, outstanding bus transaction, issue-width change or predictor algorithm change is involved.\n\nUse head and tail payload registers with a two-bit occupancy count. Each entry contains its own fetch PC, original instruction, prepared recovery address, predicted_taken and direct_aligned metadata. Decode observes the registered head directly; avoid an indexed array/head mux on its operand-address path. A successful dequeue shifts tail to head only when needed. Support simultaneous enqueue/dequeue at occupancy one and two, including a full queue replacing its consumed tail, without dropping or duplicating any instruction. Do not count an unready raw imem payload as a queued instruction. When full without dequeue, retain the fetch PC; when external imemReady is low, retain the unaccepted fetch PC. This adds a real fetch-to-decode register boundary and one initial fill cycle; it must not add an instruction bubble between steady-state dequeues.\n\nDistinguish raw instruction-bus availability from decode-head availability. Feed head availability to the existing load-use/imem-bubble hazard decisions. Dequeue exactly when decode accepts a valid head: no dequeue on a load-use bubble, backend dmem hold, M launch/busy ownership, missing head or registered recovery. When a head exists, decode may continue while io_imemReady is zero. When empty, capture invalid decode payload according to current payload/valid separation and hold until a fetch arrives. Treat the hazard output previously named stall_if as consumer backpressure; IF computes its own producer capacity and handshake independently. Preserve all existing producer eligibility, forwarding selections, load-use comparisons, retained WB data and backend transfer timing.\n\nKeep predictor lookup and B/J target preparation attached to the speculative fetch PC and current raw imem word, then enqueue their complete metadata with that word. Training remains exactly once from the advancing registered EX/MEM conditional branch. Queue entries retain the prediction made at their enqueue edge even if training changes counters before decode consumes them; EX compares against that saved prediction. Direct JAL prediction, original target reconstruction, branch mismatch predicates, conditional-branch LUI recovery and JALR effective-address recovery remain unchanged.\n\nRegistered recovery has priority over enqueue, dequeue, queue fullness, imem unavailability and the ordinary fetch PC update. Set the fetch PC to the resolving target, invalidate the entire younger queue and suppress both transfers that edge. The resident younger ID/EX instruction still receives the champion's existing side-effect and M-ownership squash; the older resolving instruction advances and retires exactly once. Reset initializes PC and occupancy and cancels every buffered instruction. Fetching a wrong-path word may fill a payload register but cannot cause a memory request, register write, predictor training or retirement before valid execution. Preserve target and memory alignment traps, default-illegal decoding, all real and ALTOPS arithmetic, memory bounds, exact top-level ports and strictly consecutive channel-0 order.\n\nThis is one structural decoupling of instruction production and consumption. Measure both bracketed cycles and all three Gowin placement options because queue registers and shift/occupancy controls cost area and the extra frontend boundary can increase recovery refill cost. Require canonical CRCs, validation literal, bounded accesses, exact marker timing and fitness above 232.91282 iterations/sec. Predict an eight-percent gain with medium confidence; queue starvation during long instruction stalls, additional refill latency after misses and fetch-control routing are the main risks. Preserve every architectural assertion in unit and fixed verification gates; update only internal test assumptions that incorrectly equate the live fetch address/payload with the queued decode head.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Add two head/tail payload registers, occupancy, producer and consumer handshakes. Buffer each fetched word with its PC and prediction/recovery metadata; decode reads the head directly. Produce independently of backend holds while space exists, support full simultaneous replacement and prevent accepting unready bus data. Give reset and registered recovery queue invalidation/PC priority. Keep predictor algorithm and registered training unchanged.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire raw io_imemReady to queue production and queue-head availability to frontend hazard control. Route consumer backpressure to queue dequeue, preserving registered recovery, the exact nret=1 memory/RVFI interface and every EX/MEM/WB producer and retirement connection.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Make the instruction-availability input mean available queued decode head. Keep original load-use source comparisons, backend payload holds, M ownership, outstanding dmem priority and validity kills. Generate consumer hold/bubble decisions independently of raw imemReady; backend stalls must not globally stop queue production.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve every architectural check. Independently model enqueue/dequeue order, head PC/instruction/prediction metadata and occupancy, replacing only obsolete live-bus-equals-decode assumptions. Cover empty/full, simultaneous transfers, instruction stalls while the head drains, filling during load-use/dmem/M holds, retained WB operands, wrong-path queue squash, back-to-back recovery, alignment traps and reset. Check exact RVFI sources/results/order, all memory effects and stable outstanding requests.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_predictor.py','description':'''Retain all counter, target, prediction and training expectations while adapting the IF interface to producer readiness and queued consumption. Verify prediction metadata stays attached to its accepted word across intervening training, queue holds and recovery, and that squashed entries never train or execute.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_fetch_queue.py','description':'''Add focused independent sequence tests for a two-entry elastic queue: every occupancy transition, full simultaneous dequeue/enqueue, prolonged consumer and producer stalls, exact payload ordering, saved predictions, recovery with both transfers possible, recovery while full or imem-unready, and reset. Require no lost or repeated accepted instructions and no stale queue entry after recovery.'''}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated structural hypothesis validates',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
