{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"agent_message","text":"The registered queue passes all eight full-RVFI cosim comparisons and canonical CoreMark CRC validation. It saves 5.2% of cycles, but its Fmax drop erases that gain. I’m refining it to fall through when empty, preserving the champion’s direct fetch path and using the queue to capture instructions during backend holds."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_54", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r9s1.yaml');h=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nh['title']='Decouple fetch with a two-entry fall-through instruction queue'\nh['motivation']='''The champion reaches 222.67 iterations/sec at 100.806 MHz and 4,527,071 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its combinational IF/ID topology couples fetch PC advancement to load-use bubbles, data-memory backpressure and owned M execution. Fetch wastes instruction-ready cycles while the backend holds, then decode bubbles on subsequent instruction-bus stalls. Independent approximately 22-percent stalls compound this loss. LESSONS.md is absent; the structural history shows that resource isolation can improve timing, whereas an extra recovery cycle without latency hiding regressed. A fully registered two-entry fetch-queue trial passed all eight full-RVFI cosim comparisons and canonical stalled CoreMark validation, saving 5.2 percent of cycles, but reached only 92.766 MHz. The final hypothesis uses fall-through to preserve immediate fetch delivery and avoid mandatory refill bubbles.'''\ns=h['hypothesis']\ns=s.replace('Insert one two-entry elastic instruction queue between', 'Insert one two-entry FALL-THROUGH elastic instruction queue between')\ns=s.replace('The fetch PC advances only when a real io_imemReady instruction is enqueued and space is available.', 'The fetch PC advances only when a real io_imemReady instruction is accepted directly by decode or stored in the queue, and capacity exists.')\ns=s.replace('Decode observes the registered head directly; avoid an indexed array/head mux on its operand-address path.', 'When occupied, decode observes the registered head directly. When empty, decode sees the current raw fetch payload and its prediction metadata through an occupancy-controlled bypass, retaining the champion\\\\'s zero-buffer-latency delivery. Never mux payload by a live hazard/redirect signal; only the registered empty flag selects the bypass, and ready controls validity.')\ns=s.replace('This adds a real fetch-to-decode register boundary and one initial fill cycle; it must not add an instruction bubble between steady-state dequeues.', 'An empty queue with ready instruction data and an accepting consumer transfers directly to decode without enqueue/dequeue or occupancy change. If decode is held instead, store that accepted fetch in the head. When occupied, preserve FIFO ordering while accepting new fetches into available tail capacity. There is no mandatory new instruction stage or initial/refill bubble; buffered entries alone cross the added storage boundary.')\ns=s.replace('Feed head availability to the existing load-use/imem-bubble hazard decisions.', 'Feed decode availability (nonempty queue OR ready direct fetch) to the existing load-use/imem-bubble hazard decisions.')\ns=s.replace('When empty, capture invalid decode payload according to current payload/valid separation and hold until a fetch arrives.', 'When empty and raw imem is unavailable, capture invalid decode payload according to current payload/valid separation and hold the unaccepted fetch PC. When empty and raw imem is ready, either deliver it directly or enqueue it during consumer backpressure, exactly once.')\ns=s.replace('and the extra frontend boundary can increase recovery refill cost', 'and the occupied/empty payload choice can lengthen decode paths')\ns=s.replace('additional refill latency after misses', 'empty/occupied selection routing')\ns=s.replace('update only internal test assumptions that incorrectly equate the live fetch address/payload with the queued decode head.', 'update only internal test assumptions that incorrectly equate the live fetch address/payload with an occupied queue head. When the queue is empty, preserve that original direct-fetch relation.')\nh['hypothesis']=s\nh['changes'][0]['description']='''Add a two-entry fall-through head/tail queue, occupancy and independent producer/consumer handshakes. Select buffered head versus the current raw fetch solely by registered occupancy; ready changes validity, never raw payload selection. Directly transfer an empty ready fetch to accepting decode without changing occupancy, and buffer it instead during backend holds. Preserve metadata and FIFO order, full simultaneous replacement, reset/recovery priority and the unchanged predictor.'''\nh['changes'][1]['description']=h['changes'][1]['description'].replace('queue-head availability', 'buffered-head OR direct-fetch availability')\nh['changes'][2]['description']=h['changes'][2]['description'].replace('available queued decode head', 'available queued head or ready direct fetch')\nh['changes'][-1]['description']+=' Verify empty direct transfer versus empty enqueue under consumer hold, PC advancement without occupancy change on bypass, transitions between buffered and bypass delivery, and no duplicate buffering of a directly consumed instruction.'\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=90));jsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()));print('Final fall-through hypothesis validates')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
