nds, branch conditions, JAL/JALR targets and misalignment\ntrap behavior. Redirect and flush only when a valid instruction can\nadvance out of EX and its resolved next PC differs from the prediction;\nthe recovery target is the resolved next PC, including PC+4 for a falsely\npredicted taken branch or a trapped target. Correctly predicted taken\nbranches and JALs retain the younger target instruction without a bubble.\nRVFI pc_wdata always uses the architectural resolution, never speculation.\n\nCompute predictions from raw imem_data independently of stall/flush\noutputs, so the load-use hazard path cannot form a combin\n…[45046 of 48118 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:07b349f4c913182f]…\nHz. That gain\nis only about 3.1 percent, below acceptance. An existing-imm precursor\nthat retained a separate post-ALU jump select reached only 101.069\nMHz, despite passing 53 formal checks, all 36 pipeline tests, eight\nRVFI cosim comparisons and canonical stalled CoreMark with unchanged\ncycles. The FINAL proposal also folds jump result selection into the\nexisting ALU_LUI path, removing the post-ALU mux from ordinary integer\nresults rather than only shortening the jump adder. Its timing remains\nto be established. A parallel-direct-adder precursor and a 32-entry\nagree table were slower, so neither is part of this proposal. The\nprincipal risks are the JALR effective-immediate select, ID operand\nrouting and the still-present main integer ALU critical path.\nMeasure all Gowin placement options. At 4,527,071 measured cycles,\nacceptance requires fitness above 232.91282 iter/sec and median\nFmax above approximately 105.442 MHz. A seven-percent clock gain\nwould clear that threshold with unchanged CoreMark throughput.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
