{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n print(d['id'], d['category'], d['outcome'], d.get('fitness'), d.get('fmax_mhz'), d.get('cycles'), d['title'])\n if d['category']=='predictor':\n  print('  Proposal:', d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:1800].replace('\\\\n',' '))\n  print('  Path:', d.get('critical_path'))\n  if d.get('implementation_notes'): print('  Notes:',d['implementation_notes'][:2500])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-0e2f75b micro_opt improvement 12.12 5.442 4491485 Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20261004-001-r1s0 micro_opt regression 12.55 5.637 4491485 Shared borrow-driven radix-4 combinational divider\nhyp-20261004-002-r1s1 structural improvement 104.26 46.833 4491817 Remove combinational division from EX with a shared iterative divider\nhyp-20261004-003-r1s2 predictor regression 12.76 5.347 4190281 Small bimodal next-PC predictor eliminates taken-branch bubbles\n  Proposal: Add one lightweight fetch-time next-PC predictor: a 64-entry untagged bimodal table of two-bit saturating conditional-branch counters, indexed by PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], plus a valid bit per entry. Cold entries use backward-taken/forward-not-taken direction until the first EX resolution initializes the counter to weakly taken or weakly not taken. On subsequent resolutions increment or decrement with saturation. Predecode only legal BRANCH funct3 values and JAL from the raw fetched instruction, construct their B/J immediate, and use one PC-relative target adder. Predict an aligned conditional target when its direction is taken, and an aligned JAL target unconditionally. All other instructions, including JALR, predict PC+4. No BTB, return stack, cache, or pipeline-depth change.  Carry the full predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through IF and ID/EX. Compute the architectural next PC in EX using the existing forwarded operands, branch conditions, JAL/JALR targets and misalignment trap behavior. Redirect and flush only when a valid instruction can advance out of EX and its resolved next PC differs from the prediction; the recovery target is the resolved next PC, including PC+4 for a falsely predicted taken branch or a trapped target. Correctly predicted taken branches and JALs retain the younger target instruction without a bubble. RVFI pc_wdata always uses the architectural resolution, never speculation.  Compute predictions from raw imem_data independently of stall/flush outputs, so the load-use hazard path cannot form a comb\n…[23731 of 26803 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8983dd33304958ad]…\nmove the fetch B/J immediate decoder and full-width direct-target adder. Carry the accepted predicted next PC with its instruction into ID/EX, holding it through backend stalls. In EX compute the architectural next PC from the original decoded immediates and forwarded operands. Compare the saved prediction against fallthrough and the direct or indirect target in parallel with branch resolution; select one-bit mismatch results with the actual condition, avoiding a late wide mux followed by equality. A target miss, stale JALR target, direction miss, or false control-flow hit is an ordinary recoverable prediction error. Every valid non-control instruction and every trap must also check for a stale taken prediction and recover to PC+4 when necessary. Full tags do not justify assuming that executable memory never changes.  Keep reco\n  Path: {'from': 'cpu/u_if/pc_8_s1', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.predicted_next_pc_25_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.966, 'arrival_ns': 19.26, 'logic_cells': 31, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
