{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/LESSONS.md')\nprint('LESSONS:', p.read_text() if p.exists() else 'absent')\nfor s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(s)\n print(d['id'],d.get('category'),d.get('outcome'), 'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),d.get('title'))\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('MOTIVATION:',d.get('motivation',''))\n  print('HYPOTHESIS:',h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "LESSONS: absent\nbaseline-bench-0e2f75b micro_opt improvement fitness 12.12 fmax 5.442 cycles 4491485 Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20261004-001-r1s0 micro_opt regression fitness 12.55 fmax 5.637 cycles 4491485 Shared borrow-driven radix-4 combinational divider\nhyp-20261004-002-r1s1 structural improvement fitness 104.26 fmax 46.833 cycles 4491817 Remove combinational division from EX with a shared iterative divider\nhyp-20261004-003-r1s2 predictor regression fitness 12.76 fmax 5.347 cycles 4190281 Small bimodal next-PC predictor eliminates taken-branch bubbles\nMOTIVATION: The baseline fetch payload is combinational, with no IF/ID register, so\nevery taken branch or jump resolved in EX discards one younger instruction\nand costs one bubble. CoreMark repeatedly executes loop and decision\nbranches, making direction prediction a direct way to reduce measured\ncycles. The reported 199.837 ns critical path passes through the existing\ncombinational M-extension ALU; a small predictor can improve iterations\nper cycle without attempting an unrelated arithmetic redesign. There are\nno recorded predictor attempts or distilled lessons for this fixture.\n\nReference replay of the canonical compiled 2K, -O3, 10-iteration CoreMark\nworkload finds 529,588 conditional branches at 158 distinct PCs, with\n294,425 taken branches, 49,367 JALs and 13,735 JALRs between the markers.\nThis 64-entry hashed table incurs 41,559 direction misses (92.15% accuracy),\nversus 111,175 for static backward-taken/forward-not-taken prediction.\nThese measurements support a small adaptive predictor rather than a\nlarger target table or static direction alone.\n\nHYPOTHESIS: Add one lightweight fetch-time next-PC predictor: a 64-entry untagged\nbimodal table of two-bit saturating conditional-branch counters, indexed\nby PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], plus a valid bit per entry. Cold entries use\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken direction until the first EX resolution\ninitializes the counter to weakly taken or weakly not taken. On subsequent\nresolutions increment or decrement\n…[68354 of 71426 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:140b2ae96f025b11]…\nned WB forwarding,\nstable memory requests, wrong-path suppression, all legality/alignment\nchecks, real and ALTOPS arithmetic, nret=1 IO and consecutive RVFI order.\nArchitectural next PCs and traps always use actual resolution.\n\nThis is one predictor storage architecture change. Its intended benefit\nis replacing the counter-flop/reset/write-enable fabric with tiny LUT\nmemories and shallow bank selection. After scrub, the predictor has the\nsame capacity and hysteresis; boot training discarded during scrub can\naffect initial state, so do not assume an identical benchmark cycle count.\nExpect eight percent fitness improvement, with low confidence: this RAM\ncandidate has not been timed, the current worst paths cross EX/bypass,\nand asynchronous RAM delay or training read-modify-write can erase gains.\nRequire unchanged formal and cosim gates, canonical stalled CoreMark CRCs\nand validation text, bounded accesses, marker-bracketed timing and scored\nmedian Gowin fitness strictly above 249.30364 iterations/sec.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
