{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n print(d['id'], d.get('category'), d.get('outcome'), 'fit',d.get('fitness'),'fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),d.get('title'))\n if d.get('category') == 'predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis')\n  print('MOTIVATION:', d.get('motivation',''))\n  print('PROPOSAL:', h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:', d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-0e2f75b micro_opt improvement fit 12.12 fmax 5.442 cycles 4491485 Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20261004-001-r1s0 micro_opt regression fit 12.55 fmax 5.637 cycles 4491485 Shared borrow-driven radix-4 combinational divider\nhyp-20261004-002-r1s1 structural improvement fit 104.26 fmax 46.833 cycles 4491817 Remove combinational division from EX with a shared iterative divider\nhyp-20261004-003-r1s2 predictor regression fit 12.76 fmax 5.347 cycles 4190281 Small bimodal next-PC predictor eliminates taken-branch bubbles\nMOTIVATION: The baseline fetch payload is combinational, with no IF/ID register, so\nevery taken branch or jump resolved in EX discards one younger instruction\nand costs one bubble. CoreMark repeatedly executes loop and decision\nbranches, making direction prediction a direct way to reduce measured\ncycles. The reported 199.837 ns critical path passes through the existing\ncombinational M-extension ALU; a small predictor can improve iterations\nper cycle without attempting an unrelated arithmetic redesign. There are\nno recorded predictor attempts or distilled lessons for this fixture.\n\nReference replay of the canonical compiled 2K, -O3, 10-iteration CoreMark\nworkload finds 529,588 conditional branches at 158 distinct PCs, with\n294,425 taken branches, 49,367 JALs and 13,735 JALRs between the markers.\nThis 64-entry hashed table incurs 41,559 direction misses (92.15% accuracy),\nversus 111,175 for static backward-taken/forward-not-taken prediction.\nThese measurements support a small adaptive predictor rather than a\nlarger target table or static direction alone.\n\nPROPOSAL: Add one lightweight fetch-time next-PC predictor: a 64-entry untagged\nbimodal table of two-bit saturating conditional-branch counters, indexed\nby PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], plus a valid bit per entry. Cold entries use\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken direction until the first EX resolution\ninitializes the counter to weakly taken or weakly not taken. On subsequent\nresolutions increment or decrement with saturation. Predecode only l\n…[75167 of 78239 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:08b6be6fd2f733e4]…\nSYSTEM checks,\nreal RV32IM and ALTOPS arithmetic, nret=1 IO and architectural RVFI.\nUse the identical indirect protocol in formal and production builds.\n\nLocal precursors for segmented fetch addresses, store-lane recovery,\nGray counters and direct recovery-operand bypass reached only 98.797,\n99.924, 105.687 and 95.323 MHz median and were rejected. The FINAL\nreturn-stack/registered-validation architecture is unimplemented and\nunmeasured. Estimate eight percent fitness improvement with low confidence.\nEven eliminating all 13,735 existing two-cycle JALR recoveries saves only\nabout 0.61 percent of 4,527,071 cycles, while each JALR adds a validation\ncycle; ordinary ALU paths may still limit Fmax. Snapshot/control routing\nand stack flops can erase the clock benefit. Measure actual bracketed\ncycles and all Gowin options; do not assume unchanged cycles. Require all\nunchanged formal/cosim gates, canonical CoreMark CRCs and validation text,\nbounded accesses, exact markers and fitness above 249.30364 iter/sec.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
