{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"command -v verilator; command -v riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc; command -v yosys; command -v sby; sed -n '1,140p' bench/programs/Makefile; sed -n '1,180p' tools/eval/formal.py; sed -n '1,140p' formal/run_all.sh\"", "aggregated_output": "/srv/hwebench/toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin/verilator\n/srv/hwebench/toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin/yosys\n/srv/hwebench/toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin/sby\nCROSS   := riscv32-unknown-elf\nCC      := $(CROSS)-gcc\n\n# CoreMark build knobs. Defaults are the canonical EEMBC reporting config\n# (2K data size, -O3); tools/eval/fpga.py's COREMARK_EXPECTED CRCs assume\n# exactly these defaults.\n# - COREMARK_SIZE: TOTAL_DATA_SIZE in coremark.h (#ifndef-guarded). 2000 is\n#   the EEMBC canonical reporting config (used by VexRiscv et al.); 6000\n#   makes the matrix/list/state working sets ~3x larger so the score is\n#   harder to inflate via register-resident working sets.\n# - COREMARK_OPT: gcc optimization. -O3 is the standard CoreMark publication\n#   flag; -O2 is what the V0 baseline used.\n# Defaults match VexRiscv's published CoreMark methodology so the\n# orchestrator's fitness number is directly comparable to public CoreMark\n# results. To override (e.g. for the prior 6K -O2 V0 baseline):\n#     make bench/programs/coremark.elf COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O2\nCOREMARK_SIZE ?= 2000\nCOREMARK_OPT  ?= -O3\n\nCFLAGS  := -march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 $(COREMARK_OPT) -static -nostartfiles \\\n           -specs=nano.specs -specs=nosys.specs \\\n           -T bench/programs/link.ld -Ibench/programs/coremark \\\n           -Ibench/programs/coremark/baremetal \\\n           -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=$(COREMARK_SIZE)\n\nSRCS := bench/programs/coremark/core_list_join.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_main.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_matrix.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_state.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_util.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/baremetal/core_portme.c \\\n        bench/programs/crt0.S\n\nbench/programs/coremark.elf: $(SRCS)\n\t$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $^ -o $@\n\nbench/programs/selftest.elf: bench/programs/selftest.S\n\t$(CC) -march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 -static -nostartfiles \\\n\t  -T bench/programs/link.ld $< -o $@\n\n# Random full-trace programs (V2). The .S files are generated once by\n# gen_random_trace.py <seed> and committed, so ev\n…[13874 of 16946 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0d6969bc48910601]…\nl $SCRIPT_DIR/riscv-formal\"\n    exit 1\nfi\n\n# OSS CAD Suite for consistent yosys / sby / bitwuzla. Homebrew yosys\n# combined with Homebrew bitwuzla has been observed to BrokenPipe inside\n# yosys-smtbmc.\nif [ -d \"$PROJECT_ROOT/.toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin\" ]; then\n    export PATH=\"$PROJECT_ROOT/.toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin:$PATH\"\nfi\n\n# Reap work dirs (and their logs) left by runs that have ended: the lock\n# file exists, is over 10 minutes old, and nobody holds it. A dir with no\n# lock file (older script versions, or one created a moment ago and not yet\n# locked) is never touched. Bounds disk growth from agents that invoke\n# run_all.sh dozens of times.\nshopt -s nullglob\nif [ -n \"$HAVE_FLOCK\" ]; then\n    for stale in \"$RISCV_FORMAL/cores/$CORE_NAME-\"*; do\n        [ \"$stale\" = \"$CORE_DIR\" ] && continue\n        lock=\"$stale/.owner.lock\"\n        [ -f \"$lock\" ] || continue\n        [ -n \"$(find \"$lock\" -mmin +10 2>/dev/null)\" ] || continue\n        if flock -n \"$lock\" true 2>/dev/null; then\n            rm -rf \"$stale\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
