{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'], d['title'])\n  print({k:d.get(k) for k in ('fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff','outcome')})\n  if d['id'].endswith('r2s2'):\n   print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n   print(d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 Small bimodal next-PC predictor eliminates taken-branch bubbles\n{'fitness': 12.76, 'fmax_mhz': 5.347, 'cycles': 4190281, 'lut4': 12530, 'ff': 684, 'outcome': 'regression'}\nhyp-20261004-003-r2s2 PC-indexed next-PC predictor with timing-isolated recovery\n{'fitness': 118.59, 'fmax_mhz': 49.908, 'cycles': 4208611, 'lut4': 3428, 'ff': 838, 'outcome': 'regression'}\nStarting from the CURRENT iterative-divider champion, implement one\ncompact fetch-time next-PC predictor: a 32-entry direct-mapped tagged\nbranch target buffer indexed by PC[6:2]. Each entry holds a valid bit,\nthe full PC[31:7] tag, the target's 30 aligned upper bits, a\nconditional-versus-unconditional bit, and a two-bit saturating direction\ncounter. A matching unconditional entry predicts its target; a matching\nconditional entry predicts its target only when the counter is taken.\nA miss predicts PC+4. Learn legal conditional branches, JAL and JALR at\nEX completion, using the resolved indirect target for JALR. Initialize\na new conditional entry weakly toward its actual outcome; subsequently\nsaturate its counter and update its PC-relative target even on not-taken\noutcomes. Jumps are always taken entries. Read the table directly from\nthe PC, independently of imem_data, decode, flush and hazard signals.\nRequire a computed target with both low bits zero before installing or\ntraining an entry, including a not-taken branch's immediate target.\nKeep target/tag storage unreset with resettable validity, allowing\ncompact asynchronous-read, synchronous-write distributed-memory\ninference; no BRAM read latency or new fetch pipeline stage is required.\n\nCarry the selected predicted next PC through IF and ID/EX. Advance the\nfetch PC along it only when the instruction is accepted, with reset\nfirst, advancing EX recovery next, and frontend hold otherwise. EX\ncomputes the same architectural next PC, link value and alignment trap\nas today. Recovery means a valid, actually advancing EX instruction's\nresolved next PC differs from its carried prediction. Redire\n…[3072 of 6144 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ef0965fa2cde79eb]…\n. It predicts 4,208,611 cycles for\nthe proposed 32-entry predictor: 6.73% more iterations/cycle. A 64-entry\nversion predicts 4,197,447 cycles, just 0.28 percentage points more\nthroughput gain for twice the target/tag storage. Prefer the smaller\ntable to limit lookup and routing costs. This is cycle-model evidence,\nnot an RTL or placed-netlist result. The 12% fitness estimate assumes\nroughly 5% Fmax improvement from recovery isolation; neither that\ntiming gain nor correctness is proven until implementation. There is\napproximately 2% Fmax-loss headroom before the modeled cycle gain falls\nbelow the acceptance margin. Target/tag lookup routing is the main risk.\nAccept only if unchanged\nformal, unit, cosim and canonical stalled CoreMark checks pass and\nmedian Fmax times bracketed iterations/cycle exceeds 104.26 by 4.6%.\n\n\nhyp-20261004-003-r3s2 Direction-only branch prediction with one-bit recovery metadata\n{'fitness': 123.45, 'fmax_mhz': 51.727, 'cycles': 4190281, 'lut4': 2885, 'ff': 809, 'outcome': 'regression'}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"Both recent predictors reduced CoreMark cycles by about 6–7%, but lost enough Fmax to miss acceptance. I’m focusing on a registered sequential-lookahead direction predictor: it moves the table lookup off the fetch-PC selection path and uses backward-taken prediction after nonsequential transfers."}}
