{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor row in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(row)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\nID:',d['id'],'TITLE:',d['title'])\n  print('RESULT:',{k:d.get(k) for k in ['fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','outcome','critical_path','implementation_notes']})\n  print('HYPOTHESIS:',d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID: hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 TITLE: Small bimodal next-PC predictor eliminates taken-branch bubbles\nRESULT: {'fitness': 12.76, 'fmax_mhz': 5.347, 'cycles': 4190281, 'outcome': 'regression', 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_0_s100', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -185.43, 'arrival_ns': 195.725, 'logic_cells': 163, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}, 'implementation_notes': ''}\nHYPOTHESIS: Add one lightweight fetch-time next-PC predictor: a 64-entry untagged\nbimodal table of two-bit saturating conditional-branch counters, indexed\nby PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], plus a valid bit per entry. Cold entries use\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken direction until the first EX resolution\ninitializes the counter to weakly taken or weakly not taken. On subsequent\nresolutions increment or decrement with saturation. Predecode only legal\nBRANCH funct3 values and JAL from the raw fetched instruction, construct\ntheir B/J immediate, and use one PC-relative target adder. Predict an\naligned conditional target when its direction is taken, and an aligned\nJAL target unconditionally. All other instructions, including JALR,\npredict PC+4. No BTB, return stack, cache, or pipeline-depth change.\n\nCarry the full predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\nIF and ID/EX. Compute the architectural next PC in EX using the existing\nforwarded operands, branch conditions, JAL/JALR targets and misalignment\ntrap behavior. Redirect and flush only when a valid instruction can\nadvance out of EX and its resolved next PC differs from the prediction;\nthe recovery target is the resolved next PC, including PC+4 for a falsely\npredicted taken branch or a trapped target. Correctly predicted taken\nbranches and JALs retain the younger target instruction without a bubble.\nRVFI pc_wdata always uses the architectural resolution, never speculation.\n\nCompute predictions from raw imem_data independently of stall/flush\noutputs, so the load-use hazard path cannot form a combinational loop.\nAdvance the PC alon\n…[20829 of 23901 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bd3b58ded69ca8f8]…\noptions 0/1/2 measured 61.943/63.030/63.030 MHz: median\n63.030 MHz, 3,027 LUT4, 883 registers, eight LUT-RAM cells and two DSPs.\nThese measurements imply fitness 140.187 iter/sec, about 7.96 percent\nabove 129.85 and above the 135.8231 acceptance threshold.\n\nThe trial also tested registered sequential-lookahead prediction and\nan ordinary two-bit table with the same early mismatch predicate. Their\nmeasured fitness was 139.052 and 136.391 respectively. Select only the\none-bit direct-lookup design described above: its modest accuracy loss\ncosts 22,365 cycles versus the two-bit trial but its smaller state and\nhigher Fmax deliver better fitness. This is measured trial evidence,\nnot an accepted implementation. Full formal and directed predictor unit\ncoverage remain required during implementation. Predictor routing and\nrecovery equivalence across alignment/hold cases are the principal risks;\nrequire every unchanged correctness gate plus scored vendor timing and\ncanonical marker-bracketed CoreMark before acceptance.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
