{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('\\\\nID:', d['id'],'TITLE:',d['title'])\n  print('RESULT:',{k:d.get(k) for k in ['outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff']})\n  print('HYPOTHESIS:',h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID: hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 TITLE: Small bimodal next-PC predictor eliminates taken-branch bubbles\nRESULT: {'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 12.76, 'fmax_mhz': 5.347, 'cycles': 4190281, 'lut4': 12530, 'ff': 684}\nHYPOTHESIS: Add one lightweight fetch-time next-PC predictor: a 64-entry untagged\nbimodal table of two-bit saturating conditional-branch counters, indexed\nby PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], plus a valid bit per entry. Cold entries use\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken direction until the first EX resolution\ninitializes the counter to weakly taken or weakly not taken. On subsequent\nresolutions increment or decrement with saturation. Predecode only legal\nBRANCH funct3 values and JAL from the raw fetched instruction, construct\ntheir B/J immediate, and use one PC-relative target adder. Predict an\naligned conditional target when its direction is taken, and an aligned\nJAL target unconditionally. All other instructions, including JALR,\npredict PC+4. No BTB, return stack, cache, or pipeline-depth change.\n\nCarry the full predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\nIF and ID/EX. Compute the architectural next PC in EX using the existing\nforwarded operands, branch conditions, JAL/JALR targets and misalignment\ntrap behavior. Redirect and flush only when a valid instruction can\nadvance out of EX and its resolved next PC differs from the prediction;\nthe recovery target is the resolved next PC, including PC+4 for a falsely\npredicted taken branch or a trapped target. Correctly predicted taken\nbranches and JALs retain the younger target instruction without a bubble.\nRVFI pc_wdata always uses the architectural resolution, never speculation.\n\nCompute predictions from raw imem_data independently of stall/flush\noutputs, so the load-use hazard path cannot form a combinational loop.\nAdvance the PC along a prediction only when fetch is actually accepted:\nno instruction-bus stall, load-use bubble, data-bus hold, or recovery.\nReset has highest priority; an EX recovery overrides an instruction-bus\nstall; otherwise the PC holds \n…[20325 of 23397 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bbcd4aa3579916f2]…\ngate and 24 existing pipeline tests. The\ntwo omitted baseline-only redirect-timing scenarios require predictor-aware\nstimulus during implementation; their architectural checks must remain.\nCanonical stalled CoreMark completed in bounds with every required CRC and the\nvalidation literal, taking 4,496,136 marker-bracketed cycles. Gowin\nplacement options 0/1/2 reached 64.214/67.947/67.947 MHz, median 67.947,\nwith 3,206 logic cells, 916 registers, eight LUT-RAMs, six BSRAMs and two\nDSPs. These measurements imply 151.12 iterations/sec, approximately\n7.37 percent above 140.75 and above the 147.2245 acceptance threshold.\nThe final worst option-0 path remains operand/ALU/memory-address logic,\n20.868 ns across 13 cells. This is trial evidence, not an accepted result;\nformal must pass again on the implementation, and full predictor-specific\ncoverage remains an implementation gate.\nAdded state and routing remain the main risks. Reimplementation must\nreproduce the median Fmax and bracketed cycles with unchanged checks.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"The prior predictors reduced CoreMark cycles, but their clock-frequency losses kept them below the champion. I’m focusing on recovery timing: prepare the recovery address in decode and use one EX branch predicate for both mismatch detection and the actual outcome. A small two-bit table should also avoid the extra misses seen with the one-bit designs."}}
