{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print('\\\\nID',d['id'],'TITLE',d['title'],'RESULT',d.get('outcome'),'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'Fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('MOTIVATION',h.get('motivation',d.get('motivation','')))\n  print('HYPOTHESIS',h.get('hypothesis',''))\n  print('NOTES',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 TITLE Small bimodal next-PC predictor eliminates taken-branch bubbles RESULT regression fitness 12.76 Fmax 5.347 cycles 4190281\nMOTIVATION The baseline fetch payload is combinational, with no IF/ID register, so\nevery taken branch or jump resolved in EX discards one younger instruction\nand costs one bubble. CoreMark repeatedly executes loop and decision\nbranches, making direction prediction a direct way to reduce measured\ncycles. The reported 199.837 ns critical path passes through the existing\ncombinational M-extension ALU; a small predictor can improve iterations\nper cycle without attempting an unrelated arithmetic redesign. There are\nno recorded predictor attempts or distilled lessons for this fixture.\n\nReference replay of the canonical compiled 2K, -O3, 10-iteration CoreMark\nworkload finds 529,588 conditional branches at 158 distinct PCs, with\n294,425 taken branches, 49,367 JALs and 13,735 JALRs between the markers.\nThis 64-entry hashed table incurs 41,559 direction misses (92.15% accuracy),\nversus 111,175 for static backward-taken/forward-not-taken prediction.\nThese measurements support a small adaptive predictor rather than a\nlarger target table or static direction alone.\n\nHYPOTHESIS Add one lightweight fetch-time next-PC predictor: a 64-entry untagged\nbimodal table of two-bit saturating conditional-branch counters, indexed\nby PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], plus a valid bit per entry. Cold entries use\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken direction until the first EX resolution\ninitializes the counter to weakly taken or weakly not taken. On subsequent\nresolutions increment or decrement with saturation. Predecode only legal\nBRANCH funct3 values and JAL from the raw fetched instruction, construct\ntheir B/J immediate, and use one PC-relative target adder. Predict an\naligned conditional target when its direction is taken, and an aligned\nJAL target unconditionally. All other instructions, including JALR,\npredict PC+4. No BTB, return stack, cache, or pipeline-depth change.\n\nCarry the full predicted nex\n…[49620 of 52692 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:383f994d554a9928]…\ne, not an accepted implementation. Reproduce the\nscore and pass all unchanged gates plus independent counter/training\nand speculative-squash coverage. At this measured cycle count, acceptance\nrequires more than 97.074 MHz and fitness above 214.43 iter/sec. Fetch\nadder/lookup routing and correct pre-edge recovery ownership remain the\nmain risks. No arithmetic algorithm or evaluation changes are proposed.\n\nThe selected trial also passed all 53 unchanged nret=1 ALTOPS formal\nchecks and all eight full-RVFI selftest/random-program cosimulation\ncomparisons, with and without both bus stalls. Arithmetic, decoder,\ndivider, multiplier, ALU service, immediate and register-file suites\npassed. Predictor-aware pipeline coverage independently models pre-edge\ncounters and advancing EX/MEM training. Adapt old redirect-cycle\nstimulus to actual misses while preserving every architectural assertion;\na predicted JAL must be replaced by a cold conditional mismatch when\na test specifically requires a squashed load payload.\n\nNOTES \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
