{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_69", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r15s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-001-r15s0\ntitle: \"Map register-file reads to native two-word groups and one write-first mux\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's explicit flop register file improved fitness substantially,\n  but its four eight-word masked-OR groups still cost read-selection LUTs\n  and routing. Several nearly tied Gowin paths involve RF reads, ALU\n  operands, forwarding and IF arithmetic, so reducing the RF read network\n  can ease placement pressure without changing the instruction schedule.\n  LESSONS.md is absent. All recorded micro_opt attempts and relevant RF\n  history were reviewed, including the losing r7 bank/bypass fusion and\n  r10 synchronous-RAM proposal. This keeps the successful local flops.\nhypothesis: |\n  Optimize only the combinational register-file read network. Retain the\n  exact 31 constant-address resettable 32-bit architectural words, x0\n  constant zero, one synchronous write port and both asynchronous reads.\n  For each port generate sixteen two-word groups. Group g is a native\n  two-input selection between words[2*g] and words[2*g+1], selected by\n  rs_addr[0]. Select the complete group using rs_addr[4:1] in an exhaustive\n  case with literal group indices. These are mux partitions with no bank\n  conflicts. Use ordinary binary mux expressions, allowing Gowin's LUT\n  and dedicated mux resources to implement the read tree; do not expand\n  the group selector into a sixteen-source masked OR.\n\n  After group selection use exactly one write-first data mux. Its grant\n  is w_en && rs_addr != 0 && w_addr == rs_addr. Select w_data on that grant,\n  otherwise the selected stored word. Remove the separate final x0 data\n  mux: reading address zero already selects literal words[0]=0, while the\n  bypass grant excludes x0 even if w_en targets x0 with nonzero data.\n  Keep bypass combinational and independent of reset, matching the current\n  same-cycle semantics even during reset. Storage still clears on reset\n  and drops writes to x0. Do not fuse bank selection and WB bypass into a\n  parallel five-so\n…[2183 of 5255 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e762b5355fb32032]…\nement; measure all three scored placements.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace four eight-word masked-OR read groups with sixteen native\n      two-word mux groups and exhaustive constant-index group selection.\n      Preserve every storage word, reset and write enable. Use one final\n      WB bypass mux per port whose grant includes nonzero read address;\n      rely on literal words[0] for x0 instead of a separate zero-result mux.\n      Preserve reset-independent write-first behavior and both read ports.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every existing reset, x0, full-address and write-first assertion.\n      Check all group boundaries and both ports simultaneously, including\n      nonzero WB data targeting x0, cross-port bypass and same-edge reset\n      with WB enabled. Compare against an independent 32-word architectural\n      reference; preserve all existing pipeline and formal/cosim gates.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
