{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_45", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r14s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-002-r14s1',\n 'title':'Pipeline low MUL through EX/MEM without global execution ownership',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 238.34 iter/sec at 107.897 MHz and 4,527,071 marker-bracketed cycles. LESSONS.md is absent; all thirteen structural attempts were reviewed. Fetch queues, synchronous register-file reads and narrow-MUL service acceleration regressed. Every low MUL currently snapshots operands, computes products, reduces them, then transfers its result while holding the entire frontend. A dedicated pipelined low-half lane can remove this structural serialization without a reorder buffer, wider retirement or a single-cycle multiplier. Temporary split-shift trials passed arithmetic-oriented cosim but lost vendor timing, so shifts are outside this final proposal.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Keep single issue, nret=1 and the existing pipeline depth, but execute ALU_MUL in a dedicated two-stage lane across the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB registers. EX captures three unsigned 16x16 products from its actual forwarded operands: ll=a[15:0]*b[15:0], lh=a[15:0]*b[31:16], hl=a[31:16]*b[15:0]. No hh product is needed for the low 32 bits. MEM forms an explicitly widened column=zero_extend(ll[31:16])+zero_extend(lh[15:0])+zero_extend(hl[15:0]); the architectural result is {column[15:0],ll[15:0]}, modulo 2\"'^32. This is exact for signed and unsigned inputs. Register products before reduction; never combine DSP multiplication and partial-product reduction in one cycle.\n\nA legal low MUL advances its ordinary instruction/RVFI metadata into EX/MEM on the product edge and retires through the existing MEM/WB register after reduction. It never launches the blocking M service or asserts divide_wait. Independent younger instructions and independent consecutive MULs can issue every cycle, while high-half multiplies and all divisions retain the existing exclusive service, snapshots, corner cases and exactly-once completion. Product registers have the same outstanding-dmem hold as EX/MEM and can capture unconditionally on other edges, keeping request/bubble/completion muxes out of their datapath. A low-MUL ownership tag is valid only for a real advancing legal MUL and is inert for reset, invalid instructions, recovery, M launch/busy and saved service completion. Keep effective-address, store-data and normal arithmetic lanes independent.\n\nTreat low MUL as a late producer like a load. Exclude it from anticipated EX/MEM complete-result forwarding without clearing its architectural reg_write or rd. Extend the existing raw-source load-use comparison to low MUL: a matching nonzero destination holds fetch and inserts a validity-only ID/EX bubble for one cycle. The retried consumer then captures the complete MEM/WB bypass choice. Retain the existing raw-source comparisons, including unused fields, because the full-RVFI cosim contract compares those operand snapshots too; this conservative interlock also avoids putting validated legality decoding on the PC hold path. Older dmem ownership still holds ID/EX and the outstanding request, older WB fields retain their forwarding value, and redirect has the original kill priority. Check MUL-to-load/store/branch/JALR/M-operation dependencies, x0, youngest-writer priority and overwrites by younger producers. The reduction must be selected before MEM/WB so retained WB forwarding always supplies the complete value.\n\nUse the original registered integer/link grants and blocking-unit result lane for every non-low-MUL instruction. On hardware low MUL those grants are all zero and no service completes, so the ordinary arithmetic_result_q is zero and the tagged pipelined product can be combined disjointly with it before the existing load-versus-integer WB register selection. Do not route the product/reduction into the ordinary early EX/MEM bypass or branch recovery path. Preserve all existing standalone ALU and multiplier arithmetic/handshake behavior; the blocking multiplier remains reachable for MULH/MULHU/MULHSU. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS retain the exact eight single-cycle substitutions, disable the real low-MUL lane/interlock, and eliminate its DSP expressions from that model. Real arithmetic and pipeline forwarding require unchanged cocotb and full-RVFI cosim checks, in addition to the fixed formal gate.\n\nThis is one structural repartition of MUL execution, with no fetch queue, predictor, shift change, cache, scoreboard or retirement-width change. Its benefit is recovered cycles from removing low-MUL service ownership; extra DSP resources and the forwarded-operand-to-product timing are the main risks. Measure actual bracketed cycles and median Gowin Fmax across all scored options. Acceptance requires fitness above 249.30364 iter/sec. Require all existing arithmetic and architectural checks, unchanged formal/cosim gates, exact canonical 2K CoreMark CRCs, validation literal, bounded accesses and marker-only timing. No measured gain is claimed until a complete trial finishes.'\"''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':7,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add an EX/MEM low-MUL ownership tag and three partial-product fields, keeping all existing architectural and retirement payloads.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Add independently held 16x16 product registers from actual forwarded operands. Route valid low MUL through ordinary advancement without blocking-service launch; exclude it from early complete-result eligibility and capture inert tags on bubbles/recovery/saved-M completion. Preserve all other execution, ownership and ALTOPS behavior.'},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Reduce registered low-MUL partial products and select the exact modulo-2\"'^32 result before MEM/WB. Retain completed results through WB holds and keep memory extraction/alignment, request strobes and non-MUL values correct.'\"'},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Extend the existing raw-source one-cycle load-use interlock to hardware low MUL. Preserve validity-only bubbling, fetch hold, memory/M ownership, redirect priority and retained WB data.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire the validated hardware low-MUL class into the late-producer interlock, tying it off only for the existing ALTOPS single-cycle substitution model. Keep exact nret=1 memory/RVFI ports and retirement order.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Preserve every architectural assertion; update only internal assumptions that every MUL owns the blocking service. Independently check full low-MUL bits, partial-product carries, all sign combinations, extrema and seeded randomized operands. Cover independent consecutive MULs, immediate/distant dependencies, raw unused-source aliases, branches/stores/JALR/loads and all real M consumers, x0, younger overwrites, bus stalls, recovery, reset, request stability and exactly-once channel-0 retirement. Retain complete ALU/multiplier suites and fixed cosim/formal gates.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated structural hypothesis; schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
