{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"agent_message","text":"The refined prototype passed all 53 formal checks and retained the cycle reduction, but its median Fmax was 97.489 MHz, below the roughly 106.702 MHz needed for acceptance. The final hypothesis keeps the pipelined MUL schedule and shares the existing multiplier’s DSP product registers instead of adding a second product bank. That sharing refinement is untested; the YAML records the measured limitation and keeps confidence low.\n"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_61", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r14s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-002-r14s1',\n 'title':'Shared DSP product bank pipelines low MUL without frontend ownership',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 238.34 iter/sec at 107.897 MHz and 4,527,071 bracketed CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent; all thirteen structural experiments were reviewed. Fetch queues and synchronous register-file reads repeatedly lost frequency. Low MUL currently serializes operand snapshot, products, reduction and result transfer while holding the entire frontend. Profiling the canonical instruction stream finds 93,960 low MULs but only 35,640 immediate consumers. Pipelining low MUL through existing execution boundaries removes unnecessary global ownership, while sharing the existing product bank avoids the additional DSP and routing cost observed in a separate-lane precursor.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Retain single issue, nret=1 and the existing pipeline depth. Reorganize the EXISTING multiplier's DSP product register bank so it serves two mutually exclusive schedules: ordinary pipelined low MUL, and the current exclusively owned MULH/MULHU/MULHSU service. Do not add a second multiplier or a duplicated EX product bank. Add an elaboration-time core-only pipelined-low-MUL option to multiplier.sv and alu.sv; their default standalone interfaces continue to provide the original blocking arithmetic and handshake behavior.\n\nFor a valid low MUL in EX, compute and register unsigned 16x16 products directly from the actual forwarded operands on the existing EX/MEM edge: ll=a[15:0]*b[15:0], lh=a[15:0]*b[31:16], hl=a[31:16]*b[15:0]. Reuse the existing ll_q/lh_q/hl_q storage and the same DSPs used by high-half multiplication; hh is irrelevant to the low result but remains available in the shared bank for high-half operations. Expose the registered low products through ALU/EX to MEM without recapturing them in another register. EX/MEM holds the low-MUL instruction metadata and ownership tag on that SAME edge. MEM computes the explicitly widened column=zero_extend(ll[31:16])+zero_extend(lh[15:0])+zero_extend(hl[15:0]); the low result is {column[15:0],ll[15:0]}, modulo 2\"'^32. This is exact for all signed and unsigned operands. Select it before the existing MEM/WB register. Never chain DSP products and reduction in one cycle, or route reduction into the early EX/MEM bypass.\n\nIn the core'\"'s pipelined mode, a shared registered control chooses product operands: use the saved a_q/b_q when the blocking multiplier is in its registered PRODUCTS phase, otherwise use live forwarded EX operands. Low MUL cannot coexist with an owned high-half operation because the latter already holds ID/EX. Preserve the original high-half request snapshot, PRODUCTS-to-REDUCE dependency, high carry and signed correction phases, stable completed result and exactly-once result consumption. REDUCE reads the pre-edge saved products; later overwriting the product bank cannot change the already registered high_base/carry. Requests, completion, reset and transitions back to ordinary low-MUL issue must not misassociate a bank value with another instruction. In default standalone mode, retain the original PRODUCTS-only capture and full blocking MUL support.\n\nThe shared bank must hold with an outstanding older dmem request, exactly like EX/MEM. Outside that hold it may capture ordinary raw operands independently of instruction validity, bubbles and M snapshot/result muxes. Real side effects depend on a separate low-MUL tag, captured only for a valid legal advancing low MUL and cleared for invalid instructions, pending recovery, blocking M launch/busy/completion and reset. Avoid introducing request/valid/completion muxes into product data. The shared bank is the partial-product register boundary; adding another bank would repeat the measured losing precursor. Confirm Gowin reuses the champion's multiplier DSP resources instead of duplicating the low lane.\n\nCapture an explicit validated is_low_mul bit beside the existing ID/EX execution classes, with the same reset/capture/hold behavior. A low MUL advances through ordinary EX/MEM metadata and never launches the blocking M service or asserts divide_wait. Independent younger instructions and independent consecutive MULs issue at one per cycle. MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU retain current ownership, saved operands, arithmetic and backpressure. The low-MUL bit drives lane ownership and its interlock without decoding a live five-bit ALU opcode in those control paths. Outside ALTOPS, anticipated complete normal EX/MEM writers exclude the registered is_multiply OR is_divide classes directly; saved blocking-service completion keeps its separate eligibility term. Actual architectural reg_write remains true for a legal low MUL.\n\nTreat low MUL as a late producer like a load. Extend the existing raw-source load-use comparisons to low MUL: a matching nonzero rd holds fetch and inserts a validity-only ID/EX bubble for one cycle. The retried consumer then selects the complete retained MEM/WB value. Retain raw comparisons, including unused fields, because full-RVFI cosim checks those source snapshots too; this conservative protocol also avoids validated legality decoding on the PC hold path. Preserve youngest-writer priority, x0, all memory/M holds, redirect kills and retained retired WB data. Low-MUL operands must come from the same actual forwarded values reported in RVFI. Illegal or squashed instructions cannot own a tag, advertise a writer, redirect, issue memory or launch a service.\n\nKeep the original ALU integer/link grants and blocking-unit result lane for every other instruction. Hardware low MUL has all integer/link grants zero and no blocking completion, so its ordinary arithmetic_result_q is zero. Combine the tagged reduced low product disjointly with that lane before the existing load-versus-integer WB selection. Preserve the independent effective-address/store-data lanes, branch recovery and every alignment/trap check. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS keep all eight exact single-cycle substitutions and normal-writer eligibility, disable the real low-MUL lane/interlock, and omit its DSP expressions. Real simulation/FPGA must use the shared pipeline. Keep standalone ALU/multiplier tests and all existing checks intact.\n\nThis is one structural repartition of multiplication, with no predictor, fetch queue, shift change, cache, reorder buffer or retirement-width change. A separate-product-bank precursor passes real lint, all eight selftest/random full-RVFI cosim comparisons with/without bus stalls and all 53 unchanged fast ALTOPS formal checks. Canonical stalled CoreMark retains every CRC, validation literal and bounded accesses: markers 15,359 and 4,295,332 give 4,279,973 cycles, a 5.46 percent reduction. However, its registered-class variant uses three DSP resources versus the champion's two, reaches 94.031/97.489/97.489 MHz (median 97.489), and scores approximately 227.78 iter/sec: a regression. The final shared-bank topology above has NOT been implemented, timed or verified; precursor correctness supports its scheduling protocol, not its new bank arbitration or a fitness gain. Sharing is essential to the final proposal, and whether it recovers sufficient timing is uncertain. At the measured cycle count acceptance above 249.30364 iter/sec requires median Fmax above 106.701285 MHz. Compare actual bracketed cycles and all three scored Gowin options. Require all original arithmetic suites, unchanged formal/full-RVFI cosim gates, canonical 2K CRCs, validation literal, bounded accesses and marker-only timing. The DSP operand mux, shared-bank fanout and existing ALU/forwarding paths are the main risks; confidence is low.''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/multiplier.sv','description':'Add a default-off core pipelined-low-MUL mode that shares the existing DSPs and ll/lh/hl/hh product registers with the blocking high-half service. Select live forwarded operands or saved PRODUCTS operands using registered service state, align bank capture/hold with EX/MEM, expose registered partial products without another bank, and preserve standalone blocking operations, high-half reduction/corrections, reset and handshakes.'},\n {'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'Expose the core-only shared product-bank mode, EX hold and registered product outputs while retaining default standalone behavior, all integer operations, blocking M ownership and exact ALTOPS substitutions. Low pipelined MUL must not request or consume the blocking service.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add validated low-MUL execution class and an EX/MEM ownership tag/partial-product view; retain architectural and retirement bundles and avoid duplicated product storage.'},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Capture explicit validated low-MUL class beside existing M classes with the same payload reset/capture/hold behavior. Preserve decoder legality, raw source snapshots, registered result grants and all invalid-payload semantics.'},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Use shared registered products as the EX/MEM low-MUL lane without recapture. Advance low-MUL metadata without global ownership, keep its tag inert on bubbles/recovery/service completion, and exclude it from early forwarding without cancelling architectural writes. Use registered classes in eligibility; preserve high-M/divide snapshots, all other execution and exact ALTOPS normal writers.'},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Reduce the shared registered products and select the exact tagged low-MUL result before MEM/WB; preserve retained complete forwarding, loads/stores/alignment, request stability and non-MUL results.'},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Extend the existing raw-source one-cycle load-use interlock to hardware low MUL. Preserve validity-only bubbling, fetch hold, backend ownership, redirect priority and retained WB data.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire validated low-MUL class, shared product hold/view and late-producer hazard through the existing pipeline; disable only the real lane/interlock for ALTOPS. Preserve exact nret=1 memory/RVFI ports and consecutive retirement order.'},\n {'file':'test/test_multiplier.py','description':'Retain all standalone arithmetic and handshake checks. Add explicit core-mode coverage of shared-bank ownership: consecutive low products, cold/warm reset, hold stability, accepted high-half requests following low products, high PRODUCTS/REDUCE overwrite timing, signed corrections, stable high results and transition back to low issue. Verify exact products/results with independent integer references and no dropped or duplicated transactions.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Preserve every architectural assertion; update only internal assumptions that every MUL owns the blocking service. Check exact low-MUL bits and source snapshots with carries, signs, extrema and seeded random operands. Cover independent/dependent consecutive MULs, raw unused-source aliases, low-to-high/high-to-low/all divide transitions, branches/stores/JALR/loads consuming products, x0, younger overwrites, simultaneous bus stalls, recovery, reset, stable requests and exactly-once ordered retirement. Keep all existing ALU/multiplier suites and fixed formal/cosim gates.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['category']=='structural' and type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Final hypothesis schema valid:',p)\noriginal=Path('cores/bench/generated/shift_trial/original'); rtl=Path('cores/bench/rtl')\nfor f in original.glob('*.sv'): (rtl/f.name).write_bytes(f.read_bytes())\nprint('Restored all temporary RTL trials.')\nPY\ngit status --short; git diff --exit-code\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
