{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261004-003-r6s2','hyp-20261004-003-r8s2','hyp-20261004-003-r12s2']:\n  h=d['hypothesis']; print(d['id']); print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-003-r6s2\nAdd one compact direction-only predictor to the current bench RTL.\nUse 64 untagged two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[7:2] XOR\nPC[13:8], initialized weakly not taken. Express storage as entry-local\nflops with constant-index write enables and an eight-by-eight grouped\ncombinational read of the prediction MSB. Update each selected entry\nlocally using its own two bits; do not read a variable-index counter\nthrough the fetch read network for read-modify-write. Lookup observes\npre-edge state, with no update bypass. The table contains direction\nonly. Predecode legal conditional BRANCH encodings and JAL from the\nraw instruction; JAL predicts taken, JALR predicts sequentially.\nBuild exact full-width B/J targets in parallel with PC+4 and suppress\ntaken predictions for misaligned direct targets.\n\nCarry predicted_taken, a preselected 32-bit direct recovery address,\nand direct-target alignment into ID/EX. Recovery address is PC+4 for\na taken prediction and the direct target otherwise. In decode register\nthe mismatch predicate branch_op XOR {2'b00,predicted_taken}, swapping\nBEQ/BNE, BLT/BGE and BLTU/BGEU. EX uses one branch predicate network\nover the existing forwarded operands to produce direction_mismatch.\nDerive the actual conditional outcome as direction_mismatch XOR the\ncarried predicted_taken bit for training, traps and architectural\nRVFI resolution. Do not instantiate a second original-predicate\ncomparator or add a full predicted-next-PC equality check. The late\nactual-outcome reconstruction must not precede the recovery-address\nmux or the advancing recovery-valid path.\n\nA valid advancing aligned conditional branch redirects only when the\nmismatch predicate is true, using the registered recovery address.\nCorrect conditional predictions and aligned JAL keep the younger\ninstruction. JALR retains forwarded rs1+immediate target calculation,\nbit-zero masking and dynamic alignment checks, and redirects to its\nactual target when it advances. Keep RVFI pc_next and link writes\narchitectural. Taken m\n…[14626 of 17698 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2f0995e34a4873c6]…\neserved/SYSTEM checks,\nreal RV32IM and ALTOPS arithmetic, nret=1 IO and architectural RVFI.\nUse the identical indirect protocol in formal and production builds.\n\nLocal precursors for segmented fetch addresses, store-lane recovery,\nGray counters and direct recovery-operand bypass reached only 98.797,\n99.924, 105.687 and 95.323 MHz median and were rejected. The FINAL\nreturn-stack/registered-validation architecture is unimplemented and\nunmeasured. Estimate eight percent fitness improvement with low confidence.\nEven eliminating all 13,735 existing two-cycle JALR recoveries saves only\nabout 0.61 percent of 4,527,071 cycles, while each JALR adds a validation\ncycle; ordinary ALU paths may still limit Fmax. Snapshot/control routing\nand stack flops can erase the clock benefit. Measure actual bracketed\ncycles and all Gowin options; do not assume unchanged cycles. Require all\nunchanged formal/cosim gates, canonical CoreMark CRCs and validation text,\nbounded accesses, exact markers and fitness above 249.30364 iter/sec.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
