{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')\nfor line in p.read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  txt=h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h\n  print('\\\\nID',d['id'],'TITLE',d['title'],'OUTCOME',d.get('outcome'),'FITNESS',d.get('fitness'),'FMAX',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'CYCLES',d.get('cycles'))\n  print(txt[:1800])\n  notes=d.get('implementation_notes','')\n  if notes: print('NOTES TAIL:',notes[-3000:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 TITLE Small bimodal next-PC predictor eliminates taken-branch bubbles OUTCOME regression FITNESS 12.76 FMAX 5.347 CYCLES 4190281\nAdd one lightweight fetch-time next-PC predictor: a 64-entry untagged\nbimodal table of two-bit saturating conditional-branch counters, indexed\nby PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8], plus a valid bit per entry. Cold entries use\nbackward-taken/forward-not-taken direction until the first EX resolution\ninitializes the counter to weakly taken or weakly not taken. On subsequent\nresolutions increment or decrement with saturation. Predecode only legal\nBRANCH funct3 values and JAL from the raw fetched instruction, construct\ntheir B/J immediate, and use one PC-relative target adder. Predict an\naligned conditional target when its direction is taken, and an aligned\nJAL target unconditionally. All other instructions, including JALR,\npredict PC+4. No BTB, return stack, cache, or pipeline-depth change.\n\nCarry the full predicted next PC with each accepted instruction through\nIF and ID/EX. Compute the architectural next PC in EX using the existing\nforwarded operands, branch conditions, JAL/JALR targets and misalignment\ntrap behavior. Redirect and flush only when a valid instruction can\nadvance out of EX and its resolved next PC differs from the prediction;\nthe recovery target is the resolved next PC, including PC+4 for a falsely\npredicted taken branch or a trapped target. Correctly predicted taken\nbranches and JALs retain the younger target instruction without a bubble.\nRVFI pc_wdata always uses the architectural resolution, never speculation.\n\nCompute predictions from raw imem_data independently of stall/flush\noutputs, so the load-use hazard path cannot form a combinational loop.\nAdvance the PC along a prediction only when fetch is actually accepted:\nno instruction-bus stall, load-use bubble, data-bus hold, or recovery.\nReset has highest priority; an EX recovery overrides an instruction-bus\nstall; o\n\nID hyp-20261004-003-r2s2 TITLE PC-indexed next-PC predictor with timing-isolated recovery O\n…[27388 of 30460 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:197d8b49d7e44866]…\ne). Reset all 64 pulses to zero.\nOverwrite every pulse at every edge: a non-event, including an older\nmemory hold, M ownership or pending recovery, clears all pulses rather\nthan retaining them. Capture actual branch_taken from the existing shared\nmismatch predicate XOR carried prediction when acceptance is true; hold\nthis one-bit payload otherwise. Reset initializes it. Pulse and outcome\nmust describe the same accepted instruction. Decode uses the full original\nhash and no workload-specific PC constants. Alignment comes from the\nretained validated direct_aligned bit, avoiding predictor-only target\narithmetic and preserving exclusion of even not-taken misaligned targets.\n\nSupply the 64 registered entry pulses and registered actual outcome to\nIF. Each local counter updates only on its own pulse, with the existing\nsaturating 00/01/10/11 transition function. Pulse bits must be one-hot or\nall-zero; implement constant-index storage/update logic rather than a\nvariable-index write array. Keep counter lookup's existi\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
