{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_93", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-003-r15s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Simultaneous row and column selection for the direction predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 64-entry bimodal predictor already removes most direct\n  control-flow bubbles. Previous tagged target tables, RAM counters, return\n  validation and training-pulse feedback lost vendor Fmax. Its current read\n  network selects eight direction groups before a second eight-way bank mux;\n  direction then controls both fetch PC and the prepared recovery operand.\n  Simultaneously decoding both halves of the lookup index can replace that\n  serial selection with a parallel entry mask and balanced reduction.\n  LESSONS.md is absent; all predictor history entries were reviewed.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change only the direction-table lookup topology. Keep the existing 64\n  entry-local two-bit saturating counters and their original binary states,\n  weakly-not-taken reset, PC[7:2] XOR PC[13:8] hash, local constant-index\n  training enables and pre-edge read semantics. Split the six-bit fetch\n  index into a three-bit row and three-bit column. Independently decode\n  each into eight one-hot wires using constant equality comparisons.\n\n  For every constant entry e, form selected_direction[e] =\n  counter_msb[e] && row_onehot[e/8] && column_onehot[e%8]. Obtain direction\n  with a balanced OR reduction of those 64 terms. Both row and column\n  selection enter before reduction; remove the existing eight group\n  results followed by the case-selected bank mux. Describe the reduction\n  as an associative bit-vector reduction or explicit balanced four-input\n  tree, never a serial accumulator loop. Keep constant entry wiring and\n  shared row/column predecode so neither comparison is replicated 64 times.\n  The intended FPGA mapping